#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * Input-realism MEASUREMENT (#109, epic #94 — behavioral realism audit).
 *
 * WHAT IT PROVES. Every vendor on the target list profiles *behavior*: whether an event was
 * generated by the user agent (`isTrusted`), whether a keystroke produced the full
 * keydown→keypress→beforeinput→input→keyup sequence PER CHARACTER, how the inter-keystroke
 * intervals are distributed, and whether the pointer travelled to the element or teleported onto
 * it. Every stealth check this repo owns is FINGERPRINT-shaped (navigator/WebGL/UA surfaces); none
 * of them has ever looked at what our own input verbs emit. This script is the first look. It
 * drives the REAL MCP verb path — the stdio launcher, the real policy engine, the real egress
 * filter, `browser_click` / `browser_type` / `browser_select_option` / `browser_press_key` — at a
 * local fixture that records, server-side, every DOM event those verbs actually produce, and then
 * answers four questions with numbers:
 *
 *   Q1 trustedness   — is `isTrusted === true` on every event our verbs generate? Answered PER VERB
 *                      AND PER TARGET TYPE, never as one whole-run fraction: the driver splits
 *                      `fill()` by input type (a text field takes the CDP insertText path and is
 *                      trusted; a date/range/color field takes a DIRECT `.value =` assignment plus
 *                      dispatched Events and is NOT), so a single aggregate number over a fixture
 *                      that only had a text field would report a clean "all trusted" while a real
 *                      consumer typing into a date field gets untrusted events. The fixture
 *                      therefore carries one input from EACH branch.
 *   Q2 completeness  — per-character key sequence, or one value-set plus a single `input`?
 *                      does a click produce mousedown→mouseup→click, with any movement first?
 *                      does the `submit: true` leg — which appends a real `press("Enter")` — emit
 *                      the keystroke the fill body does not?
 *   Q3 timing        — inter-event interval distribution (min/median/max/mean/stddev/variance),
 *                      plus DWELL (mousedown→mouseup, keydown→keyup), not one sample.
 *   Q4 trajectory    — does the pointer move, and along what path? (headful-vs-headless recorded.)
 *
 * AND THEN IT JUDGES. Measuring without saying whether the answer is good enough leaves the reader
 * to supply the threshold, and every reader supplies a different one. A final section states, per
 * question, ACCEPTABLE / NOT-ACCEPTABLE / INDETERMINATE against rules written down next to the
 * thresholds they use, and hands a single explicit SHOULD-OPEN / CLOSE-AS-NOT-NEEDED decision to
 * #110 (the conditional timing model). "Acceptable" there always means "not obviously synthetic on
 * the axes this local fixture can see" — never "undetectable"; a fixture is not a vendor.
 *
 * THIS IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A GATE. It exits non-zero ONLY when the INSTRUMENT failed — the
 * fixture was unreachable, the capture path could not be proven, an input verb errored so its phase
 * measures nothing, or the negative control could not be distinguished from the real run. An
 * unflattering finding (untrusted events, zero keystrokes, a teleporting pointer) is reported and
 * exits 0: that is data, and #111 is the gate that will act on it. Nothing here changes production
 * behavior; #109 explicitly forbids fixing what it finds.
 *
 * THE THREE RUNS. The whole design exists so that "we measured nothing" can never be printed as
 * "our verbs emit nothing" — the single most damaging finding this epic could produce is also the
 * exact shape of a broken instrument, so each is proven separately before any answer is licensed:
 *
 *   run 1  REAL      the verbs under test, through the real MCP path.
 *   run 2  SELF-TEST the page dispatches exactly one synthetic event of EVERY captured type. This
 *                    proves, per event type, that the listener + POST + server path can SEE that
 *                    type. Without it a blind keyboard channel reads identically to a keystroke-free
 *                    verb path, and the harness would publish "no keystrokes at all" as a finding.
 *                    Each question is gated on the channels IT reads, never on a global OR — a
 *                    separating mouse axis must not license a keyboard-channel answer.
 *   run 3  CONTROL   the negative control: the SAME elements driven from page script the way a naive
 *                    "stealth browser" does — `el.click()`, direct `.value` assignment plus a
 *                    dispatched `input`/`change`, and a dispatched `KeyboardEvent` sequence. Its
 *                    expected shape is ASSERTED (exact counts + a completion marker), so a control
 *                    that threw partway cannot pass by producing a zero that looks like a discriminator.
 *
 * HOW IT REACHES THE FIXTURE THROUGH THE REAL POLICY LAYER. The egress filter blocks loopback as
 * anti-SSRF, but it is PURE — it never resolves DNS — so a synthetic hostname that is not an IP
 * literal and does not end in `.localhost` / `.internal` / `.local` passes it. We map
 * `input-fixture.example` (the `.example` TLD is reserved and never resolves publicly) to 127.0.0.1
 * in /etc/hosts, bind the fixture on 127.0.0.1, and allowlist that host for the spawned launcher.
 * The real filter and the real allowlist stay intact — strictly better evidence than weakening
 * them. (This is a property of the instrument, not a security claim: a green run here says nothing
 * about whether loopback is reachable through the gateway in deployment, where the container network
 * filter is the complementary layer.) If /etc/hosts is not writable (a non-root local run), the
 * script falls back to an in-process server built with the TEST-ONLY `policyEgress` hook (precedent:
 * scripts/validate-vault-host-login.mjs) and SAYS SO LOUDLY in the report, because the two paths are
 * not equivalent evidence: the fallback measures the same INPUT path but skips the real launcher
 * process and the real egress filter. The /etc/hosts edit is reverted on the normal path AND on
 * SIGINT/SIGTERM, so a Ctrl-C'd local run does not leave the mapping behind.
 *
 * RUN IT
 *   in-container (the reading that counts — headful Chrome under Xvfb, the shipping vehicle):
 *     docker build --platform linux/amd64 -f docker/Dockerfile -t browse-gateway:realism .
 *     docker run --rm --platform linux/amd64 --shm-size=1g --init browse-gateway:realism \
 *       node scripts/measure-input-realism.mjs
 *   locally (needs a built dist/; without root the /etc/hosts step fails and it takes the fallback):
 *     npm run build && node scripts/measure-input-realism.mjs
 *
 * ENV
 *   INPUT_REALISM_OUT=<path>    write the full structured record as JSON here (default: no file)
 *   INPUT_REALISM_MODE=auto|stdio|inprocess   force a path (default auto: stdio, fallback in-process)
 *   INPUT_REALISM_TEXT=<string> the string browser_type sends (default "Blue Widget 7" — mixed
 *                               case, a digit and spaces, so per-character completeness is visible)
 *   INPUT_REALISM_KEY=<key>     the key browser_press_key sends (default "9")
 *   INPUT_REALISM_FLUSH_MS=<n>  per-phase quiescence wait, ms (default 500; the page debounces 200)
 *   INPUT_REALISM_HOST=<host>   override the synthetic fixture hostname (default input-fixture.example)
 *   BGW_CHANNEL / BGW_NO_SANDBOX / BGW_HEADLESS / DISPLAY   passed through to the browser as usual
 */
import http from "node:http";
import dns from "node:dns/promises";
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync, mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, rmSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { dirname, join } from "node:path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "node:url";
import { randomBytes } from "node:crypto";
import { Client } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js";
import { StdioClientTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/stdio.js";

// --- configuration -------------------------------------------------------------------------
const FIXTURE_HOST = process.env.INPUT_REALISM_HOST || "input-fixture.example";
const HOSTS_PATH = "/etc/hosts";
const FIXTURE_PATH = "/fixture";
const REPORT_PATH = "/report";
/** The typed string. Mixed case + a digit + spaces exercises shifted and unshifted keys so a
 *  per-character sequence is visible; deliberately nothing that reads like a credential. */
const TEXT = process.env.INPUT_REALISM_TEXT || "Blue Widget 7";
const PRESS_KEY = process.env.INPUT_REALISM_KEY || "9";
const SELECT_VALUE = "bravo";

/**
 * "Travelled" threshold for Q4, in CSS pixels: the widest separation between any two points on a
 * click's pointer approach must reach this for the click to count as having travelled.
 *
 * Module scope because the trajectory is computed well before the ACCEPT table is built, and
 * because the number is the whole substance of the question — a reader looking for "what counts as
 * travel" should find it without reading the judgement code. ACCEPT.minPointerSpanPx aliases it.
 */
const MIN_POINTER_SPAN_PX = 8;
/**
 * The two values driven into the SET-VALUE branch of `fill()`. Deliberately NOT env-overridable: the
 * driver validates these against the input type and THROWS on a malformed one ("2026/08/14", "abc"),
 * which would surface as an errored verb — i.e. an empty phase — and an empty phase is read by this
 * instrument as "the untrusted branch could not be measured". An operator typo must not be able to
 * silently delete the highest-signal measurement in the ticket, so these are fixed in source.
 */
const DATE_VALUE = "2026-08-14";
const RANGE_VALUE = "70";
/** Page-side debounce before a batch is POSTed back; the harness waits longer than this per phase. */
const PAGE_FLUSH_MS = 200;
const FLUSH_MS = Number(process.env.INPUT_REALISM_FLUSH_MS || 500);
/** How many quiescence waits a phase gets before it gives up and says so. */
const SETTLE_ROUNDS = 4;
/** Hard cap on recorded events so a pathological mousemove storm can't grow the page unbounded. */
const MAX_EVENTS = 6000;
const MODE = process.env.INPUT_REALISM_MODE || "auto";
const MODES = ["auto", "stdio", "inprocess"];
const CALL_TIMEOUT_MS = 120_000;
const CONSUMER_ID = "input-realism";
/** Printed rows per phase before the table elides — the JSON record always carries every event. */
const MAX_TABLE_ROWS = 48;

/** The event types the fixture listens for, in CAPTURING phase so non-bubbling focus/blur/scroll are
 *  seen too. `beforeinput` is an addition to the #109 list: real editing fires it and a naive
 *  `.value = x` + dispatched `input` does not, so it is a cheap extra discriminator.
 *  `scroll` is here because `loc.click()` runs scrollIntoViewIfNeeded before it presses, and
 *  behavioral vendors read scroll as its own channel — a click that scrolls the page into position
 *  looks different from one that does not. It costs one capture listener that only appends to the
 *  same buffer every other type appends to, so it cannot perturb what the verbs emit; the only risk
 *  is volume (a smooth-scroll storm), and MAX_EVENTS already bounds that with a reported drop count.
 *  Recorded but NOT a discriminator: this fixture is one screen tall, so an empty scroll column here
 *  means "nothing needed scrolling", not "the verbs never scroll". */
const EVENT_TYPES = [
  "mousedown", "mouseup", "click", "mousemove",
  "pointerdown", "pointerup", "pointermove",
  "keydown", "keypress", "keyup",
  "beforeinput", "input", "change",
  "focus", "blur", "scroll",
];

/**
 * Malformed instrument configuration is refused BEFORE anything is measured, because every one of
 * these mistakes degrades into a plausible-looking measurement rather than an error:
 *   - a non-numeric FLUSH_MS serializes as JSON `null`, every `browser_wait_for` is rejected by its
 *     schema, no phase ever waits out the page's debounce, and every phase records zero events;
 *   - an empty/one-char TEXT makes the per-character acceptance rule pass VACUOUSLY. That rule is
 *     `keydown >= TEXT.length * ACCEPT.keydownsPerChar`, so at TEXT="" it reads `0 >= 0` and a fill
 *     that emitted no keystroke whatsoever is judged "PER-CHARACTER keystroke sequence" — which then
 *     hands #110 a CLOSE-AS-NOT-NEEDED built on zero measured keystrokes, the single most damaging
 *     wrong answer this instrument can produce. It also sets `controlExpected.keyEvents`
 *     (3*(TEXT.length + PRESS_KEY.length)), so a degenerate TEXT shrinks the negative control's
 *     asserted shape to something a half-run control could hit by accident;
 *   - a typo'd MODE ("in-process") silently means `auto`, so an operator who asked for the weaker
 *     fallback path may get the stdio path and quote it as the stronger evidence.
 */
const configErrors = [];
if (!Number.isFinite(FLUSH_MS) || FLUSH_MS <= 0) configErrors.push(`INPUT_REALISM_FLUSH_MS must be a positive number, got ${JSON.stringify(process.env.INPUT_REALISM_FLUSH_MS)}`);
if (TEXT.length < 2) configErrors.push(`INPUT_REALISM_TEXT must be at least 2 characters (it sets the per-character discriminator threshold), got ${JSON.stringify(TEXT)}`);
if (PRESS_KEY.length < 1) configErrors.push("INPUT_REALISM_KEY must be a non-empty key name");
if (!MODES.includes(MODE)) configErrors.push(`INPUT_REALISM_MODE must be one of ${MODES.join("|")}, got ${JSON.stringify(MODE)}`);
if (configErrors.length) {
  console.error("=== INSTRUMENT FAILED (configuration) ===");
  for (const e of configErrors) console.error(`  ${e}`);
  process.exit(1);
}

/** A control-flow abort raised when a self-check makes everything downstream meaningless (the
 *  fixture never loaded, nothing was captured). Distinguished from a genuine crash so the catch
 *  block can print a one-line reason instead of a stack that reads like a harness bug. */
class InstrumentAbort extends Error {}

let instrumentFailures = 0;
const check = (label, ok, detail = "") => {
  console.log(`  ${ok ? "PASS" : "FAIL"}  ${label}${detail ? ` — ${detail}` : ""}`);
  if (!ok) instrumentFailures++;
};
const note = (msg) => console.log(`  note  ${msg}`);
const r3 = (x) => (typeof x === "number" && Number.isFinite(x) ? Math.round(x * 1000) / 1000 : null);

// --- the fixture page ----------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * The instrumentation, as a self-contained IIFE handed to the page verbatim. It carries NO `${`
 * interpolation (its configuration arrives through the embedded JSON `<script>` block) — the same
 * discipline FINGERPRINT_COLLECTOR_JS keeps in src/browser/fingerprint.ts, so a future edit can't
 * accidentally splice harness state into page script.
 *
 * It buffers into a monotonically-indexed array and POSTs the un-acknowledged tail on a debounce.
 * `sent` only advances on a successful POST, so a dropped batch is retried rather than lost, and
 * the server can dedupe and detect gaps from the per-event index. Reading the log SERVER-SIDE is
 * deliberate: pulling it through an aria snapshot would both mangle the structured data and
 * perturb the very page whose event stream is the measurement.
 */
const PAGE_JS = `
(function () {
  var cfg = JSON.parse(document.getElementById("realism-config").textContent);
  var EVENTS = [];
  // Out-of-band channels that ride the same POST: "marks" carry run-to-completion evidence for the
  // page-driven routines, "errors" carry the reason one of them stopped early. Both exist because a
  // routine that threw halfway leaves counts that look exactly like a deliberate zero.
  var marks = [];
  var errors = [];
  var sent = 0;
  var sentMarks = 0;
  var sentErrors = 0;
  var sentDropped = 0;
  var dropped = 0;
  var seq = 0;
  // A per-PAGE-LOAD nonce. Phase attribution slices by this page's monotonic event index, which is
  // only monotonic WITHIN one load: a second load under the same run label restarts seq at 0, would
  // overwrite earlier indices, and would leave every later phase reading as empty — a silent, total
  // loss of the measurement. Reporting the nonce lets the harness DETECT that instead of assert it.
  var load = String(Date.now()) + "-" + String(Math.random()).slice(2);
  var origin = performance.now();
  var timer = null;
  var inflight = false;

  function idOf(t) {
    if (t === window) return "#window";
    if (t === document) return "#document";
    if (!t) return "#none";
    if (t.id) return "#" + t.id;
    return "<" + String(t.nodeName || "?").toLowerCase() + ">";
  }

  function record(e) {
    if (EVENTS.length >= cfg.maxEvents) {
      // Count the loss AND schedule a POST for it. The drop counter rides the request body, so
      // without this the last flush acknowledges every event, pending() goes false, and every
      // subsequent drop is invisible — the report would then understate how truncated the log is,
      // which reads downstream as "our verbs emitted fewer events than they did".
      dropped++;
      schedule();
      return;
    }
    var t = e.target;
    var rec = {
      i: seq++,
      type: e.type,
      isTrusted: e.isTrusted === true,
      eventTs: typeof e.timeStamp === "number" ? e.timeStamp : null,
      obsMs: performance.now() - origin,
      target: idOf(t)
    };
    if (typeof e.clientX === "number") {
      rec.clientX = e.clientX;
      rec.clientY = e.clientY;
      rec.screenX = typeof e.screenX === "number" ? e.screenX : null;
      rec.screenY = typeof e.screenY === "number" ? e.screenY : null;
      rec.button = typeof e.button === "number" ? e.button : null;
      rec.buttons = typeof e.buttons === "number" ? e.buttons : null;
      if (typeof e.pointerType === "string") rec.pointerType = e.pointerType;
      if (typeof e.isPrimary === "boolean") rec.isPrimary = e.isPrimary;
    }
    if (e.type.indexOf("key") === 0) {
      rec.key = e.key;
      rec.code = e.code;
      rec.keyCode = e.keyCode;
      rec.ctrlKey = e.ctrlKey === true;
      rec.altKey = e.altKey === true;
      rec.shiftKey = e.shiftKey === true;
      rec.metaKey = e.metaKey === true;
      rec.repeat = e.repeat === true;
    }
    if (e.type === "input" || e.type === "beforeinput" || e.type === "change") {
      rec.inputType = typeof e.inputType === "string" ? e.inputType : null;
      rec.data = typeof e.data === "string" ? e.data : null;
      rec.valueLength = t && typeof t.value === "string" ? t.value.length : null;
    }
    EVENTS.push(rec);
    schedule();
  }

  function pending() {
    return sent < EVENTS.length || sentMarks < marks.length || sentErrors < errors.length || sentDropped !== dropped;
  }

  function schedule() {
    if (timer !== null || inflight) return;
    timer = setTimeout(flush, cfg.flushMs);
  }

  function flush() {
    timer = null;
    if (inflight || !pending()) return;
    var uptoEvents = EVENTS.length;
    var uptoMarks = marks.length;
    var uptoErrors = errors.length;
    var atDropped = dropped;
    var body = JSON.stringify({
      run: cfg.run,
      load: load,
      dropped: atDropped,
      from: sent,
      events: EVENTS.slice(sent, uptoEvents),
      marks: marks.slice(sentMarks, uptoMarks),
      errors: errors.slice(sentErrors, uptoErrors)
    });
    inflight = true;
    fetch(cfg.reportUrl, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      body: body,
      cache: "no-store"
    }).then(function () {
      sent = uptoEvents;
      sentMarks = uptoMarks;
      sentErrors = uptoErrors;
      sentDropped = atDropped;
    }, function () {
      // leave the cursors where they are so the next flush retries this exact slice: a lost batch
      // would silently shorten a phase and read as "our verbs emitted fewer events than they did".
    }).then(function () {
      inflight = false;
      if (pending()) schedule();
    });
  }

  // Last-chance delivery if the page is torn down mid-measurement (a submit-driven navigation, a
  // session close) — otherwise the tail of the final phase is simply missing from the record.
  function beacon() {
    if (!pending()) return;
    try {
      var payload = JSON.stringify({
        run: cfg.run,
        load: load,
        dropped: dropped,
        from: sent,
        events: EVENTS.slice(sent),
        marks: marks.slice(sentMarks),
        errors: errors.slice(sentErrors)
      });
      if (navigator.sendBeacon(cfg.reportUrl, new Blob([payload], { type: "application/json" }))) {
        sent = EVENTS.length;
        sentMarks = marks.length;
        sentErrors = errors.length;
        sentDropped = dropped;
      }
    } catch (err) { /* nothing left to do at teardown */ }
  }
  window.addEventListener("pagehide", beacon, true);
  document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", beacon, true);
  // Safety net for a schedule() that was never reached (a listener that threw, a dropped timer).
  setInterval(function () { if (pending()) schedule(); }, cfg.flushMs * 5);

  for (var i = 0; i < cfg.types.length; i++) {
    document.addEventListener(cfg.types[i], record, true);
  }

  // The submit:true leg of browser_type appends a real press("Enter") into a text field. If that
  // Enter ever reached a form submission the page would NAVIGATE mid-measurement: the load nonce
  // would change, the monotonic event index would restart at 0, every index already recorded would
  // be overwritten by the new load's, and the phases after it would read as empty — a silent, total
  // truncation of the capture that looks exactly like "the verbs stopped emitting". The fixture
  // deliberately has no <form> at all, so nothing should submit; this listener is the belt to that
  // suspenders, so a later edit that wraps the field in a form cannot quietly break the instrument.
  document.addEventListener("submit", function (e) { e.preventDefault(); }, true);

  /**
   * Run a page-driven routine and REPORT whether it finished. A bare sequence that throws partway
   * (a renamed element id, a constructor the browser rejects) records everything before the throw
   * and nothing after, with no signal — and "the leg never ran" is indistinguishable from "the leg
   * ran and emitted zero", which is precisely the reading this instrument must never fabricate.
   */
  function runGuarded(name, fn) {
    marks.push(name + ":start");
    try {
      fn();
      marks.push(name + ":done");
    } catch (err) {
      errors.push(name + ": " + String((err && err.message) || err));
    }
    schedule();
  }

  // --- run 2: the capture self-test ---------------------------------------------------------
  // Dispatch exactly ONE synthetic event of every captured type. Whatever arrives at the server
  // proves, per type, that the listener + POST + storage path can see that type at all. This is the
  // only thing that makes a zero in the real run readable: without it, a blind keyboard channel and
  // a keystroke-free verb path produce the identical table, and the harness would publish the
  // second reading. It runs on its own page load under its own run label so it cannot contribute a
  // single event to the negative control's counts.
  function synthFor(type) {
    var init = { bubbles: true, cancelable: true };
    if (type.indexOf("pointer") === 0) return new PointerEvent(type, init);
    if (type.indexOf("mouse") === 0 || type === "click") return new MouseEvent(type, init);
    if (type.indexOf("key") === 0) return new KeyboardEvent(type, init);
    if (type === "beforeinput" || type === "input") return new InputEvent(type, init);
    if (type === "focus" || type === "blur") return new FocusEvent(type, { bubbles: false });
    return new Event(type, init);
  }

  function runSelfTest() {
    var field = document.getElementById("realism-field");
    for (var k = 0; k < cfg.types.length; k++) {
      var type = cfg.types[k];
      try {
        field.dispatchEvent(synthFor(type));
      } catch (err) {
        // Per-type, not per-run: one unconstructable type must not hide the other fourteen.
        errors.push("selftest " + type + ": " + String((err && err.message) || err));
      }
    }
  }

  // --- run 3: the negative control ------------------------------------------------------------
  // Page script driving the SAME elements the real verbs drive, in the shape a naive automation
  // layer uses: a direct value assignment plus dispatched Events, dispatched KeyboardEvents that
  // change nothing, and el.click() (which emits a lone "click" with no mousedown/mouseup and no
  // pointer movement at all). Every event here is dispatchEvent-created, so every one of them is
  // isTrusted === false.
  function dispatchKeys(el, text) {
    for (var k = 0; k < text.length; k++) {
      var ch = text.charAt(k);
      var init = { key: ch, code: "Key" + ch.toUpperCase(), bubbles: true, cancelable: true };
      el.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent("keydown", init));
      el.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent("keypress", init));
      el.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent("keyup", init));
    }
  }

  function runSynthetic() {
    var field = document.getElementById("realism-field");
    var button = document.getElementById("realism-button");
    var choice = document.getElementById("realism-choice");
    // Deliberately NO field.focus(). el.focus() makes the USER AGENT fire the focus event, and a
    // UA-fired event carries isTrusted === true — inside the one run whose whole job is to be 100%
    // page-generated. A trusted event here erodes the trustedFraction discriminator silently, in the
    // file whose subject is trustedness, and the assignment below needs no focus to take effect.
    field.value = cfg.text;
    field.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", { bubbles: true }));
    field.dispatchEvent(new Event("change", { bubbles: true }));
    dispatchKeys(field, cfg.text);
    button.click();
    choice.value = cfg.selectValue;
    choice.dispatchEvent(new Event("change", { bubbles: true }));
    dispatchKeys(field, cfg.pressKey);
  }

  // Let the navigate's own settle finish before either routine fires, so its events are attributable
  // to the routine rather than racing the navigation the harness is still awaiting.
  if (cfg.selftest) setTimeout(function () { runGuarded("selftest", runSelfTest); }, cfg.routineDelayMs);
  if (cfg.synthetic) setTimeout(function () { runGuarded("control", runSynthetic); }, cfg.routineDelayMs);
})();
`;

/** The fixture document. Copy is deliberately neutral: nothing here may collide with the
 *  BLOCK_PHRASES / PX_BLOCK_PHRASES the drive path scans for, or navFailed would classify our own
 *  fixture as an anti-bot interstitial and the navigate would throw instead of measuring. */
function fixtureHtml(cfg) {
  // `<` is escaped even though JSON.stringify already quotes the payload: JSON escapes neither `<`
  // nor `/`, so an env-supplied string containing `</script>` would close this block early, break
  // JSON.parse in the page, install no listeners at all, and surface as "no events reached the
  // fixture" — sending the operator after the browser/hosts path instead of their own env var.
  const json = JSON.stringify(cfg).replace(/</g, "\\u003c");
  return `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>input realism fixture</title></head>
<body>
<h1>input realism fixture</h1>
<p>A local measurement page. It records the DOM events it receives and posts them back to the
harness that served it. Nothing on this page contacts any other host.</p>
<p><label for="realism-field">realism text field</label>
<input id="realism-field" name="realism-field" type="text" aria-label="realism text field" autocomplete="off"></p>
<!-- The date and range fields are NOT decoration. The driver's injected fill() splits on input type:
     the text field above lands in the TYPE-INTO set and is completed over CDP Input.insertText (a
     trusted input event), while {color,date,time,datetime-local,month,range,week} land in the
     SET-VALUE set and are completed by assigning element.value and DISPATCHING Event("input") /
     Event("change") from page context — which are isTrusted === false. A fixture carrying only a
     text field lets Q1 print "every event is trusted" while a consumer filling a date field is
     emitting the single most legible synthetic-input tell there is, so both branches are on the
     page and Q1 is reported per target type. "date" is the clearest case (a valid value is
     unambiguous); "range" is the cheap numeric variant, kept because a one-element sample would
     leave "is this a quirk of date inputs?" unanswerable. Nothing here is a form field of any
     consequence — neutral names, no form element, no action. -->
<p><label for="realism-date">realism date field</label>
<input id="realism-date" name="realism-date" type="date" aria-label="realism date field"></p>
<p><label for="realism-range">realism range slider</label>
<input id="realism-range" name="realism-range" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="10" aria-label="realism range slider"></p>
<p><button id="realism-button" type="button">realism action button</button></p>
<p><label for="realism-choice">realism choice</label>
<select id="realism-choice" name="realism-choice" aria-label="realism choice">
<option value="alpha">alpha</option>
<option value="bravo">bravo</option>
<option value="charlie">charlie</option>
</select></p>
<script id="realism-config" type="application/json">${json}</script>
<script>${PAGE_JS}</script>
</body>
</html>`;
}

// --- the fixture server (the event log lives HERE, not in the page) ---------------------------
/** run label -> { byIndex: Map<i, event>, dropped, posts, loads: Set, marks: Set, errors: [] } */
const runs = new Map();
const bucketFor = (run) => {
  let b = runs.get(run);
  if (!b) {
    b = { byIndex: new Map(), dropped: 0, posts: 0, loads: new Set(), marks: new Set(), errors: [] };
    runs.set(run, b);
  }
  return b;
};
/** Dense, index-ordered event list for a run, plus how many indices never arrived. */
function eventsOf(run) {
  const b = runs.get(run);
  if (!b) return { events: [], missing: 0, dropped: 0, posts: 0, loads: [], marks: [], errors: [] };
  const keys = [...b.byIndex.keys()].sort((a, b2) => a - b2);
  const highest = keys.length ? keys[keys.length - 1] : -1;
  return {
    events: keys.map((k) => b.byIndex.get(k)),
    missing: highest + 1 - keys.length,
    dropped: b.dropped,
    posts: b.posts,
    loads: [...b.loads],
    marks: [...b.marks],
    errors: b.errors.slice(),
  };
}
/** Highest index seen so far — the phase-slice cursor. Indices are monotonic per page load, so
 *  slicing by index (not by array length) stays correct even if a batch arrives out of order. */
const highestIndex = (run) => {
  const b = runs.get(run);
  if (!b || b.byIndex.size === 0) return -1;
  return Math.max(...b.byIndex.keys());
};

function readBody(req, capBytes = 4 * 1024 * 1024) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    let size = 0;
    const chunks = [];
    req.on("data", (c) => {
      size += c.length;
      if (size > capBytes) {
        reject(new Error("report body over cap"));
        req.destroy();
        return;
      }
      chunks.push(c);
    });
    req.on("end", () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks).toString("utf8")));
    req.on("error", reject);
  });
}

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  // The Host header carries the synthetic fixture hostname; route on path only.
  let url;
  try {
    url = new URL(req.url ?? "/", "http://fixture.invalid");
  } catch {
    res.writeHead(400);
    return res.end("bad request");
  }
  if (req.method === "POST" && url.pathname === REPORT_PATH) {
    void readBody(req)
      .then((raw) => {
        const payload = JSON.parse(raw);
        const b = bucketFor(String(payload.run ?? "unknown"));
        b.posts++;
        b.dropped = Math.max(b.dropped, Number(payload.dropped) || 0);
        if (typeof payload.load === "string") b.loads.add(payload.load);
        for (const m of payload.marks ?? []) if (typeof m === "string") b.marks.add(m);
        for (const e of payload.errors ?? []) if (typeof e === "string" && !b.errors.includes(e)) b.errors.push(e);
        for (const ev of payload.events ?? []) {
          if (typeof ev?.i === "number") b.byIndex.set(ev.i, ev); // idempotent: a retried batch overwrites
        }
        res.writeHead(204);
        res.end();
      })
      .catch(() => {
        // An over-cap body already destroyed the request socket, so guard the reply: throwing here
        // would surface as an unhandled rejection and kill the harness mid-measurement.
        if (!res.writableEnded) {
          try {
            res.writeHead(400);
            res.end("bad report");
          } catch { /* socket already gone */ }
        }
      });
    return;
  }
  if (req.method === "GET" && url.pathname === FIXTURE_PATH) {
    const run = url.searchParams.get("run") || "real";
    const cfg = {
      run,
      synthetic: url.searchParams.get("synthetic") === "1",
      selftest: url.searchParams.get("selftest") === "1",
      reportUrl: REPORT_PATH,
      types: EVENT_TYPES,
      flushMs: PAGE_FLUSH_MS,
      maxEvents: MAX_EVENTS,
      text: TEXT,
      pressKey: PRESS_KEY,
      selectValue: SELECT_VALUE,
      routineDelayMs: 250,
    };
    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/html; charset=utf-8", "Cache-Control": "no-store" });
    return res.end(fixtureHtml(cfg));
  }
  res.writeHead(404, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
  res.end("not found");
});

// --- /etc/hosts: make the synthetic host resolve to the loopback fixture ----------------------
/**
 * Append `127.0.0.1 <FIXTURE_HOST>` if it is not already present. The container runs as root, so
 * this normally succeeds; a local non-root run fails here and the caller takes the documented
 * in-process fallback. We keep the original bytes so the exit handler can restore the file
 * byte-for-byte — and only when the file still looks exactly like "what we wrote", so a concurrent
 * edit is never clobbered.
 */
function ensureHostsEntry() {
  const line = `127.0.0.1 ${FIXTURE_HOST}\n`;
  // The host is env-overridable, so escape it before it becomes a pattern — an unescaped `.` would
  // make `input-fixtureXexample` satisfy the "already present" test and skip the write we need.
  const hostPattern = FIXTURE_HOST.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&");
  // `[^\S\n]` is "whitespace except newline", used instead of `\s` deliberately: `\s` matches `\n`,
  // so a `\s`-separated pattern under the `m` flag can start on the `127.0.0.1 localhost` line and
  // walk ACROSS newlines to reach the fixture host bound to a DIFFERENT address further down. That
  // reads as "already present", skips the write, resolves to the wrong IP, and drops the run to the
  // fallback while reporting the resolution as the cause instead of the declined write.
  const alreadyRe = new RegExp(`^[^\\S\\n]*127\\.0\\.0\\.1(?:[^\\S\\n]+\\S+)*[^\\S\\n]+${hostPattern}[^\\S\\n]*$`, "m");
  try {
    const original = readFileSync(HOSTS_PATH, "utf8");
    if (alreadyRe.test(original)) return { ok: true, added: false, original, written: original };
    const written = original.endsWith("\n") ? original + line : `${original}\n${line}`;
    writeFileSync(HOSTS_PATH, written);
    return { ok: true, added: true, original, written };
  } catch (err) {
    return { ok: false, added: false, error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err) };
  }
}

function restoreHostsEntry(state) {
  if (!state?.added) return;
  try {
    // Only revert what we can prove is still our own edit — never rewrite someone else's file.
    if (readFileSync(HOSTS_PATH, "utf8") === state.written) writeFileSync(HOSTS_PATH, state.original);
  } catch {
    /* best-effort; the container is disposable and a left-behind line is inert */
  }
}

// --- MCP plumbing ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
const textOf = (res) => res?.content?.map((c) => (c.type === "text" ? c.text : "")).join("") ?? "";

/** Spawn the REAL stdio launcher and talk to it exactly as a consumer agent does. */
async function connectStdio(token) {
  const launcher = fileURLToPath(new URL("../dist/mcp/main.js", import.meta.url));
  const transport = new StdioClientTransport({
    command: "node",
    args: [launcher],
    env: {
      ...process.env,
      BGW_MCP_CONSUMER_ID: CONSUMER_ID,
      BGW_MCP_CONSUMER_TOKEN: token,
      BGW_MCP_ALLOWLIST: FIXTURE_HOST,
      // Neutralize deployment posture that would perturb the measurement: a warm (vault) open would
      // measure a restored session rather than the cold input path, and a proxy escalation would put
      // an exit between the verbs and a loopback fixture that cannot be reached through one.
      BGW_VAULT_DIR: "",
      BGW_ON_DATACENTER_IP: "0",
      BGW_FORCE_PROXY_HOSTS: "",
      BGW_WARMUP_HOSTS: "",
      BGW_DIAG_VERIFY_EGRESS: "0",
    },
    stderr: "inherit",
  });
  const client = new Client({ name: "input-realism", version: "1.0.0" });
  await client.connect(transport);
  return { client, close: () => client.close().catch(() => {}) };
}

/**
 * Documented fallback: build the same MCP server in-process over a gateway whose PolicyEngine takes
 * the TEST-ONLY `policyEgress` override, and connect an in-memory transport pair. The INPUT path
 * (BrowserCore.click/type/selectOption/pressKey — what #109 measures) is byte-for-byte the same;
 * what this loses is the real launcher process and the real egress filter, so its evidence is
 * weaker and the report says so.
 */
async function connectInProcess(token) {
  const { buildGatewayRuntime } = await import("../dist/mcp/runtime.js");
  const { GatewayDriveController } = await import("../dist/mcp/drive-controller.js");
  const { createGatewayMcpServer } = await import("../dist/mcp/server.js");
  const { Allowlist, tokenEnvKey } = await import("../dist/policy/index.js");
  const { InMemoryTransport } = await import("@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/inMemory.js");

  const root = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "bgw-input-realism-"));
  const manifestPath = join(root, "consumers.json");
  writeFileSync(manifestPath, JSON.stringify([{ id: CONSUMER_ID, allow: ["127.0.0.1"] }]));
  const env = {
    PATH: process.env.PATH,
    HOME: process.env.HOME,
    ...(process.env.DISPLAY ? { DISPLAY: process.env.DISPLAY } : {}),
    BGW_CHANNEL: process.env.BGW_CHANNEL ?? "chrome",
    ...(process.env.BGW_NO_SANDBOX ? { BGW_NO_SANDBOX: process.env.BGW_NO_SANDBOX } : {}),
    ...(process.env.BGW_HEADLESS ? { BGW_HEADLESS: process.env.BGW_HEADLESS } : {}),
    BGW_CONSUMERS_MANIFEST: manifestPath,
    [tokenEnvKey(CONSUMER_ID)]: token,
    BGW_MAX_SESSIONS: "3",
  };
  const runtime = buildGatewayRuntime(env, {
    log: (m) => process.stderr.write(`[measure-input-realism] ${m}\n`),
    policyEgress: () => false,
  });
  const drive = new GatewayDriveController(runtime.gateway, runtime.secrets, token, {
    onDatacenterIp: false,
    allowlist: new Allowlist(["127.0.0.1"]),
    consumerId: CONSUMER_ID,
    vault: null,
    timeouts: runtime.config.timeouts,
  });
  const server_ = createGatewayMcpServer({
    version: "0.1.0",
    drive,
    // #109 exercises the interactive verbs only; a retrieve call here would be a harness bug, so
    // make it loud rather than silently measuring a path this instrument does not model.
    retrieve: async () => {
      throw new Error("retrieve is not exercised by the input-realism instrument");
    },
  });
  const [clientTransport, serverTransport] = InMemoryTransport.createLinkedPair();
  await server_.connect(serverTransport);
  const client = new Client({ name: "input-realism", version: "1.0.0" });
  await client.connect(clientTransport);
  return {
    client,
    close: async () => {
      await client.close().catch(() => {});
      await runtime.gateway.shutdown().catch(() => {});
      rmSync(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
    },
  };
}

// --- analysis helpers --------------------------------------------------------------------------
function summarize(values) {
  if (!values.length) return null;
  const s = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const n = s.length;
  const mean = s.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / n;
  const variance = s.reduce((a, b) => a + (b - mean) ** 2, 0) / n;
  const median = n % 2 ? s[(n - 1) / 2] : (s[n / 2 - 1] + s[n / 2]) / 2;
  // `variance` is emitted alongside `stddev` rather than left implicit in it. #94 names variance as
  // one of the statistics the timing answer owes, and a reader who wants it should not have to trust
  // that the stddev printed here is the population form (÷n) rather than the sample form (÷n-1) and
  // square it themselves — that guess is wrong half the time and silently rescales the answer.
  return { n, min: r3(s[0]), median: r3(median), max: r3(s[n - 1]), mean: r3(mean), stddev: r3(Math.sqrt(variance)), variance: r3(variance) };
}

const countOf = (events, ...types) => events.filter((e) => types.includes(e.type)).length;

/**
 * The interval between two specific events, in ms — the primitive behind every DWELL statistic
 * (mousedown→mouseup on a click, keydown→keyup on a keystroke), which epic #94 names explicitly and
 * which no aggregate inter-event delta can substitute for: dwell is a WITHIN-gesture duration, and
 * the inter-event series mixes it with the gaps BETWEEN gestures.
 *
 * Both readings must come off the same clock. `Event.timeStamp` and the listener's
 * `performance.now()` have different time origins, so pairing one of each would add the offset
 * between those origins to the dwell and manufacture an outlier in exactly the statistic being read.
 * `obsMs` is always present, so falling back to it for BOTH events is always available and always
 * self-consistent.
 */
function pairIntervalMs(a, b) {
  if (!a || !b) return null;
  if (typeof a.eventTs === "number" && typeof b.eventTs === "number") return b.eventTs - a.eventTs;
  if (typeof a.obsMs === "number" && typeof b.obsMs === "number") return b.obsMs - a.obsMs;
  return null;
}

/** mousedown→mouseup dwell for each press in `events`, matched by the FIRST mouseup after each
 *  mousedown. Unmatched downs (a press whose release landed in the next phase's slice) are skipped
 *  rather than paired with a distant release, which would report a dwell of hundreds of ms. */
function pointerDwells(events) {
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < events.length; i++) {
    if (events[i].type !== "mousedown") continue;
    const up = events.slice(i + 1).find((e) => e.type === "mouseup");
    const dt = pairIntervalMs(events[i], up);
    if (dt !== null && dt >= 0) out.push(dt);
  }
  return out;
}

/** keydown→keyup dwell per key, matched on `code` so an overlapping key (a held modifier plus a
 *  character key) pairs with its OWN release rather than with whichever release came first. */
function keyDwells(events) {
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < events.length; i++) {
    const d = events[i];
    if (d.type !== "keydown") continue;
    const up = events.slice(i + 1).find((e) => e.type === "keyup" && e.code === d.code);
    const dt = pairIntervalMs(d, up);
    if (dt !== null && dt >= 0) out.push(dt);
  }
  return out;
}

/**
 * Inter-event intervals from the browser's own event timestamps. The two available clocks
 * (`Event.timeStamp`, relative to the document's time origin, and the listener's `performance.now()`
 * reading) have DIFFERENT origins, so they are chosen once for the whole series rather than per
 * event — mixing them would inject the offset between the two origins into a single delta and
 * produce a fabricated outlier in exactly the statistic this ticket exists to read.
 */
function deltas(events) {
  const useEventTs = events.every((e) => typeof e.eventTs === "number");
  const base = events.map((e) => (useEventTs ? e.eventTs : e.obsMs));
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 1; i < base.length; i++) out.push(base[i] - base[i - 1]);
  return out;
}

function detailOf(e) {
  if (e.type.startsWith("key")) {
    const mods = ["ctrl", "alt", "shift", "meta"].filter((m) => e[`${m}Key`]);
    return `key=${JSON.stringify(e.key ?? null)} code=${e.code ?? "-"} keyCode=${e.keyCode ?? "-"}${mods.length ? ` mods=${mods.join("+")}` : ""}`;
  }
  if (e.type === "input" || e.type === "beforeinput" || e.type === "change") {
    return `inputType=${e.inputType ?? "-"} data=${JSON.stringify(e.data ?? null)} valueLen=${e.valueLength ?? "-"}`;
  }
  if (typeof e.clientX === "number") {
    return `client=(${e.clientX},${e.clientY}) screen=(${e.screenX},${e.screenY}) button=${e.button ?? "-"} buttons=${e.buttons ?? "-"}${e.pointerType ? ` pointerType=${e.pointerType}` : ""}`;
  }
  return "";
}

function printEventTable(events) {
  if (!events.length) {
    console.log("      (no events)");
    return;
  }
  const d = deltas(events);
  console.log(`      ${"#".padStart(4)}  ${"event".padEnd(13)} ${"trusted".padEnd(7)} ${"dt(ms)".padStart(9)}  ${"target".padEnd(17)} detail`);
  const shown = events.slice(0, MAX_TABLE_ROWS);
  shown.forEach((e, i) => {
    const dt = i === 0 ? "-" : String(r3(d[i - 1]));
    console.log(
      `      ${String(e.i).padStart(4)}  ${e.type.padEnd(13)} ${(e.isTrusted ? "true" : "FALSE").padEnd(7)} ${dt.padStart(9)}  ${String(e.target).padEnd(17)} ${detailOf(e)}`,
    );
  });
  if (events.length > shown.length) console.log(`      ... ${events.length - shown.length} more (full list in the JSON record)`);
}

/** Pull a snapshot ref for an element by matching its accessible name in the aria tree. Falls back
 *  to a CSS selector — the verbs accept both — and the report records WHICH was used, because a run
 *  driven by refs is the shape a real consumer agent actually produces. */
function refFor(tree, name) {
  for (const line of tree.split("\n")) {
    if (!line.includes(`"${name}"`)) continue;
    const m = /\[ref=([a-z]?\d*e\d+)\]/i.exec(line);
    if (m) return m[1];
  }
  return null;
}

// --- main ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
console.log("=== browse-gateway :: input-realism measurement (#109 / epic #94) ===");
console.log("    A MEASUREMENT, not a gate: it exits non-zero only if the INSTRUMENT failed.");

const startedAt = new Date().toISOString();
const token = `tok-${randomBytes(24).toString("hex")}`;
await new Promise((r) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", r));
const PORT = server.address().port;

const hostsState = MODE === "inprocess" ? { ok: false, error: "INPUT_REALISM_MODE=inprocess" } : ensureHostsEntry();
// The finally block only runs on the normal path; a signal kills the process straight past it. A
// local run that is Ctrl-C'd would then leave `127.0.0.1 <fixture host>` behind in the OPERATOR's
// /etc/hosts permanently. Restore first, then re-raise so the exit status still reads as "killed".
for (const sig of ["SIGINT", "SIGTERM"]) {
  process.once(sig, () => {
    restoreHostsEntry(hostsState);
    process.removeAllListeners(sig);
    process.kill(process.pid, sig);
  });
}
let resolvedTo = null;
if (hostsState.ok) {
  resolvedTo = await dns.lookup(FIXTURE_HOST).then((a) => a.address, () => null);
}
const useStdio = MODE === "stdio" || (MODE !== "inprocess" && hostsState.ok && resolvedTo === "127.0.0.1");
const BASE = useStdio ? `http://${FIXTURE_HOST}:${PORT}` : `http://127.0.0.1:${PORT}`;

console.log("");
console.log("--- transport ------------------------------------------------------------------");
if (useStdio) {
  const hostsNote = hostsState.ok
    ? `/etc/hosts ${hostsState.added ? "line added" : "line already present"}, resolves to ${resolvedTo}`
    : `/etc/hosts NOT written (${hostsState.error}) — forced by INPUT_REALISM_MODE=stdio, so ${FIXTURE_HOST} may not resolve`;
  console.log(`  path: REAL stdio launcher (dist/mcp/main.js) — real PolicyEngine, real egress filter`);
  console.log(`  fixture: ${BASE}${FIXTURE_PATH}  (${hostsNote})`);
} else {
  console.log(`  path: IN-PROCESS FALLBACK — createGatewayMcpServer over a gateway with policyEgress: () => false`);
  console.log(`  reason: ${MODE === "inprocess" ? "forced by INPUT_REALISM_MODE" : hostsState.ok ? `${FIXTURE_HOST} did not resolve to 127.0.0.1 (got ${resolvedTo})` : `/etc/hosts not writable: ${hostsState.error}`}`);
  console.log(`  fixture: ${BASE}${FIXTURE_PATH}`);
  console.log("  !! NOT EQUIVALENT EVIDENCE to the stdio path. The INPUT path (BrowserCore click/type/");
  console.log("  !! selectOption/pressKey) is identical, so Q1-Q4 still measure the real verbs — but the");
  console.log("  !! real launcher process and the real egress filter were bypassed to reach loopback.");
}
const headless = process.env.BGW_HEADLESS === "1";
console.log(`  browser: channel=${process.env.BGW_CHANNEL || "chrome (default)"} headless=${headless} DISPLAY=${process.env.DISPLAY ?? "(unset)"} platform=${process.platform}`);
console.log(`  typed string: ${JSON.stringify(TEXT)}   press key: ${JSON.stringify(PRESS_KEY)}   select value: ${JSON.stringify(SELECT_VALUE)}`);

const record = {
  ticket: "#109",
  epic: "#94",
  startedAt,
  transport: useStdio ? "stdio-launcher" : "in-process-policyEgress-override",
  transportCaveat: useStdio ? null : "policyEgress override — real egress filter and real launcher process bypassed",
  browser: { channel: process.env.BGW_CHANNEL || "chrome", headless, display: process.env.DISPLAY ?? null, platform: process.platform },
  input: { text: TEXT, pressKey: PRESS_KEY, selectValue: SELECT_VALUE },
  targeting: {},
  phases: [],
  runs: {},
  capture: {},
  control: {},
  questions: {},
  separation: {},
  instrumentFailures: [],
};
const fail = (msg) => {
  record.instrumentFailures.push(msg);
};
/** A check whose failure also lands in the machine-readable failure list. */
const mustPass = (label, ok, detail, failMsg) => {
  check(label, ok, detail);
  if (!ok) fail(failMsg ?? label);
  return ok;
};

let conn = null;
try {
  conn = useStdio ? await connectStdio(token) : await connectInProcess(token);
  const client = conn.client;
  const call = (name, args = {}) => client.callTool({ name, arguments: args }, undefined, { timeout: CALL_TIMEOUT_MS });

  /**
   * Wait until the page has stopped delivering events for this run: issue browser_wait_for
   * (the real verb, so even the waiting rides the verb path) and repeat while new events keep
   * arriving. Bounded, so a page that never quiesces cannot hang the measurement.
   *
   * Returns null ONLY when the page genuinely went quiet. Falling out of the bounded loop with
   * events still arriving returns a reason string instead: those two outcomes used to be the same
   * `null`, which made an un-drained phase indistinguishable from a drained one — and every phase
   * boundary downstream assumes the previous phase drained.
   */
  const settle = async (run) => {
    // ALWAYS wait at least once, and only stop once a whole wait has passed with nothing new. A
    // "stop when the count is unchanged" loop that checks BEFORE waiting would exit immediately on a
    // phase whose events are still sitting in the page's 200ms debounce — and record it as producing
    // no events at all, which is exactly the kind of false negative this ticket must not manufacture.
    for (let i = 0; i < SETTLE_ROUNDS; i++) {
      const before = highestIndex(run);
      const res = await call("browser_wait_for", { timeMs: FLUSH_MS });
      if (res.isError) return `the quiescence wait itself errored: ${textOf(res).replace(/\s+/g, " ").slice(0, 160)}`;
      if (highestIndex(run) === before) return null;
    }
    return `events still arriving after ${SETTLE_ROUNDS} waits of ${FLUSH_MS}ms — phase attribution past this point is unreliable`;
  };

  /**
   * Run one verb and open a phase at the page's current event index. The slice itself is deferred:
   * `startI` is stored and every phase is cut from the FINAL event log after the last unload flush
   * (see resliceRun). Materializing the slice here instead would drop any event that was recorded
   * during this phase but POSTed after this moment — its index is below the next phase's start, so
   * it belongs to neither slice and vanishes from the analysis while still counting in the run
   * totals, leaving the per-phase and whole-run numbers silently disagreeing.
   */
  const phase = async (run, label, fn, meta = {}) => {
    const startI = highestIndex(run) + 1;
    const res = await fn();
    const settleError = await settle(run);
    const entry = {
      run,
      label,
      startI,
      // `verb` + `targetKind` are carried on the entry rather than re-derived from the label by
      // string matching. Q1 has to be reported PER VERB and PER TARGET TYPE (a text field and a date
      // field go down different branches of fill() and only one of them is trusted), and a label
      // regex that silently stops matching after someone renames a phase would collapse those
      // buckets back into the whole-run average this ticket exists to stop quoting.
      verb: meta.verb ?? null,
      targetKind: meta.targetKind ?? null,
      ...meta,
      isError: res?.isError === true,
      text: textOf(res).slice(0, 400),
      settleError,
      events: [],
    };
    record.phases.push(entry);
    console.log(
      `  [${label}]${entry.isError ? "  *** VERB RETURNED AN ERROR ***" : ""}${settleError ? "  *** DID NOT QUIESCE ***" : ""}`,
    );
    if (entry.isError) console.log(`      error: ${entry.text.replace(/\s+/g, " ").slice(0, 300)}`);
    if (settleError) console.log(`      settle: ${settleError}`);
    return entry;
  };

  /**
   * Cut every phase of a run from the final event log. Boundaries are the phases' own start indices,
   * so an event lands in the phase that was open when the PAGE recorded it, regardless of when the
   * batch carrying it arrived. `endI` closes the last phase: for the real run that is the teardown
   * boundary, so the unload tail (a blur, and a change if the field's value moved) is reported as
   * its own bucket instead of being folded into the last verb's slice and read as part of what that
   * verb emits.
   */
  const resliceRun = (run, endI) => {
    const evs = eventsOf(run).events;
    const entries = record.phases.filter((p) => p.run === run);
    entries.forEach((p, idx) => {
      const upper = idx + 1 < entries.length ? entries[idx + 1].startI : endI;
      p.events = evs.filter((e) => e.i >= p.startI && e.i < upper);
    });
    return { entries, tail: evs.filter((e) => e.i >= endI) };
  };

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // RUN 1 — the real verb path.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- run 1: REAL verb path ------------------------------------------------------");

  const nav = await phase("real", "browser_navigate", () => call("browser_navigate", { url: `${BASE}${FIXTURE_PATH}?run=real` }));
  check("fixture reachable through the real verb path", !nav.isError, nav.isError ? nav.text.replace(/\s+/g, " ").slice(0, 200) : `${BASE}${FIXTURE_PATH}`);
  if (nav.isError) {
    fail("navigate to the fixture failed — nothing downstream can be measured");
    throw new InstrumentAbort(
      useStdio
        ? `the fixture at ${BASE} was unreachable through the real launcher (is ${FIXTURE_HOST} resolvable inside the browser?)`
        : `the fixture at ${BASE} was unreachable through the in-process path`,
    );
  }

  let snapText = "";
  await phase("real", "browser_snapshot (ref discovery)", async () => {
    // The aria tree is captured in the closure rather than off the phase entry: the entry keeps only
    // a 400-char excerpt of a verb's reply so the JSON record cannot balloon with page snapshots.
    const res = await call("browser_snapshot", {});
    snapText = textOf(res);
    return res;
  });
  const fieldRef = refFor(snapText, "realism text field");
  const buttonRef = refFor(snapText, "realism action button");
  const choiceRef = refFor(snapText, "realism choice");
  const dateRef = refFor(snapText, "realism date field");
  const rangeRef = refFor(snapText, "realism range slider");
  const fieldTarget = fieldRef ?? "#realism-field";
  const buttonTarget = buttonRef ?? "#realism-button";
  const choiceTarget = choiceRef ?? "#realism-choice";
  const dateTarget = dateRef ?? "#realism-date";
  const rangeTarget = rangeRef ?? "#realism-range";
  record.targeting = {
    field: { ref: fieldRef, used: fieldTarget },
    button: { ref: buttonRef, used: buttonTarget },
    choice: { ref: choiceRef, used: choiceTarget },
    date: { ref: dateRef, used: dateTarget },
    range: { ref: rangeRef, used: rangeTarget },
  };
  // A caveat, not an instrument failure: the verbs accept a raw selector as well as a ref, so the
  // measurement still runs. It changes what the run REPRESENTS though — a real consumer drives by
  // ref off a snapshot — so it is surfaced and recorded rather than silently swapped in.
  const allRefs = Boolean(fieldRef && buttonRef && choiceRef && dateRef && rangeRef);
  note(`targeting: field=${fieldRef ?? "#realism-field (CSS fallback)"} button=${buttonRef ?? "#realism-button (CSS fallback)"} choice=${choiceRef ?? "#realism-choice (CSS fallback)"} date=${dateRef ?? "#realism-date (CSS fallback)"} range=${rangeRef ?? "#realism-range (CSS fallback)"}`);
  if (!allRefs) {
    note("at least one snapshot ref did not resolve — that leg was driven by CSS selector, which is a");
    note("slightly different shape from what a consumer agent produces. Re-check the aria tree.");
  }
  record.targeting.allRefsResolved = allRefs;

  // Each phase registers itself on record.phases; the analysis below reads them back from there
  // (re-sliced against the FINAL event log), so nothing is held in a local array that could drift
  // out of sync with the entries the re-slice actually updates.
  await phase("real", "browser_click (text field)", () => call("browser_click", { target: fieldTarget, element: "realism text field" }), { verb: "browser_click", targetKind: "input[type=text]" });
  await phase("real", "browser_type (text field, submit:false)", () => call("browser_type", { target: fieldTarget, element: "realism text field", text: TEXT, submit: false }), { verb: "browser_type", targetKind: "input[type=text]" });
  // The submit:true leg is a SEPARATE phase, not a flag on the one above, because the two halves of
  // it are structurally different code paths and averaging them would hide the interesting half:
  // the fill body goes through CDP Input.insertText and emits no keystroke at all, while `submit`
  // appends a genuine press("Enter"). Every consumer that drives a search box takes this leg, and
  // measuring only submit:false would let the report say "our typing emits no keys" without ever
  // noticing that the one key it does emit arrives through a completely different mechanism.
  const submitEntry = await phase("real", "browser_type (text field, submit:true)", () => call("browser_type", { target: fieldTarget, element: "realism text field", text: TEXT, submit: true }), { verb: "browser_type+submit", targetKind: "input[type=text]" });
  // The SET-VALUE branch of fill(). These two phases are the reason the fixture grew a date and a
  // range input: per the driver's own source they never reach the keyboard at all — value is
  // assigned directly and `input`/`change` are dispatched from page context — so they are the one
  // place in the real run where an isTrusted === false is EXPECTED. If they come back trusted, that
  // is a finding in the other direction and equally worth knowing.
  const dateEntry = await phase("real", "browser_type (date field — set-value branch)", () => call("browser_type", { target: dateTarget, element: "realism date field", text: DATE_VALUE, submit: false }), { verb: "browser_type", targetKind: "input[type=date]" });
  const rangeEntry = await phase("real", "browser_type (range slider — set-value branch)", () => call("browser_type", { target: rangeTarget, element: "realism range slider", text: RANGE_VALUE, submit: false }), { verb: "browser_type", targetKind: "input[type=range]" });
  await phase("real", "browser_click (action button)", () => call("browser_click", { target: buttonTarget, element: "realism action button" }), { verb: "browser_click", targetKind: "button" });
  await phase("real", "browser_select_option", () => call("browser_select_option", { target: choiceTarget, element: "realism choice", values: [SELECT_VALUE] }), { verb: "browser_select_option", targetKind: "select" });
  await phase("real", "browser_click (text field, second visit)", () => call("browser_click", { target: fieldTarget, element: "realism text field" }), { verb: "browser_click", targetKind: "input[type=text]" });
  await phase("real", "browser_press_key", () => call("browser_press_key", { key: PRESS_KEY }), { verb: "browser_press_key", targetKind: "input[type=text] (focused)" });

  // Captured BEFORE navigating away: everything at or above this index was produced while the page
  // was being torn down, not by a verb under test.
  const teardownStartI = highestIndex("real") + 1;

  {
    const soFar = eventsOf("real");
    check("the real run captured events at all", soFar.events.length > 0, `${soFar.events.length} event(s) over ${soFar.posts} POST(s)`);
    if (soFar.events.length === 0) {
      fail("zero events captured on the real path — the instrument measured nothing");
      throw new InstrumentAbort("no events reached the fixture server from the real verb path");
    }
  }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // RUN 2 — the capture self-test. Proves, per event type, that this instrument can SEE it.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- run 2: CAPTURE SELF-TEST ---------------------------------------------------");
  const selfNav = await phase("selftest", "browser_navigate (?selftest=1)", () =>
    call("browser_navigate", { url: `${BASE}${FIXTURE_PATH}?run=selftest&selftest=1` }),
  );
  // Recorded as a failure in its own right, not left to surface as "blind to every event type":
  // a self-test page that never loaded and a capture path that sees nothing produce the same empty
  // bucket, and only the first of those is a navigation problem.
  mustPass(
    "self-test page loaded",
    !selfNav.isError,
    selfNav.isError ? selfNav.text.replace(/\s+/g, " ").slice(0, 200) : "",
    "the capture self-test page did not load — capture cannot be proven for any event type",
  );
  await settle("selftest");

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // RUN 3 — the negative control. Same page, same elements, driven from page script.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- run 3: NEGATIVE CONTROL (page-script synthesis) -----------------------------");
  const synthNav = await phase("synthetic", "browser_navigate (?synthetic=1)", () =>
    call("browser_navigate", { url: `${BASE}${FIXTURE_PATH}?run=synthetic&synthetic=1` }),
  );
  mustPass(
    "control page loaded",
    !synthNav.isError,
    synthNav.isError ? synthNav.text.replace(/\s+/g, " ").slice(0, 200) : "",
    "the negative-control page did not load — the comparison would be vacuous",
  );
  // The control drives itself; the harness only waits, so nothing it does can contaminate the control.
  await settle("synthetic");

  // One last navigation whose ONLY job is to fire `pagehide` on the control page, so its sendBeacon
  // delivers any tail the debounced POSTs had not acknowledged. Each run in turn is flushed by the
  // navigation that follows it; without this the control — whose exact event counts are asserted
  // below — would be the one run read while a batch could still be in flight, and a short read there
  // fails the assertion as if the control had crashed.
  await phase("drain", "browser_navigate (unload flush)", () =>
    call("browser_navigate", { url: `${BASE}${FIXTURE_PATH}?run=drain` }),
  );

  const realRun = eventsOf("real");
  const selfRun = eventsOf("selftest");
  const synthRun = eventsOf("synthetic");
  record.runs.real = realRun;
  record.runs.selftest = selfRun;
  record.runs.synthetic = synthRun;

  const { entries: realEntries, tail: teardownEvents } = resliceRun("real", teardownStartI);
  resliceRun("selftest", Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY);
  resliceRun("synthetic", Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY);
  resliceRun("drain", Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY);

  for (const [label, run] of [["real", realRun], ["control", synthRun], ["self-test", selfRun]]) {
    if (run.missing > 0 || run.dropped > 0) {
      note(`${label} event log is incomplete: ${run.missing} index gap(s), ${run.dropped} dropped at the page cap — its counts are LOWER BOUNDS.`);
    }
  }

  console.log("");
  console.log("--- run 1 event log (final attribution, after the unload flush) ------------------");
  for (const p of realEntries) {
    console.log("");
    console.log(`  [${p.label}]${p.isError ? "  *** VERB RETURNED AN ERROR ***" : ""}${p.settleError ? "  *** DID NOT QUIESCE ***" : ""}  ${p.events.length} event(s)`);
    printEventTable(p.events);
  }
  if (teardownEvents.length) {
    console.log("");
    console.log(`  [teardown — page unload, NOT attributable to a verb] ${teardownEvents.length} event(s)`);
    printEventTable(teardownEvents);
  }

  const R = realRun.events;
  const S = synthRun.events;
  const SELF = selfRun.events;
  // Phases are looked up by the (verb, targetKind) metadata they were opened with, never by parsing
  // their printed label. Three phases now start with "browser_type" and only one of them is the
  // plain text-field fill this ticket's per-character question is about; a startsWith() lookup would
  // have picked whichever came first and quietly answered Q2/Q3 for a different input type.
  const findPhase = (verb, targetKind = null) =>
    realEntries.find((p) => p.verb === verb && (targetKind === null || p.targetKind === targetKind)) ?? null;
  const typeEntry = findPhase("browser_type", "input[type=text]");
  const keyEntry = findPhase("browser_press_key");
  const selectEntry = findPhase("browser_select_option");
  const clickEntries = realEntries.filter((p) => p.verb === "browser_click");
  const typePhase = typeEntry?.events ?? [];
  const keyPhase = keyEntry?.events ?? [];
  const selectPhase = selectEntry?.events ?? [];
  const submitPhase = submitEntry?.events ?? [];
  const datePhase = dateEntry?.events ?? [];
  const rangePhase = rangeEntry?.events ?? [];
  const clickPhases = clickEntries.map((p) => p.events);
  /** Every phase that drove an input verb. Deliberately excludes browser_navigate, browser_snapshot
   *  and the teardown tail: those are the instrument's own traffic, and folding them into a
   *  trustedness fraction dilutes it with events no consumer's verb ever produced. */
  const verbEntries = realEntries.filter((p) => p.verb);

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // INSTRUMENT HEALTH. Everything here is about the instrument, not about the gateway: each check
  // rules out one way a zero in the tables below could mean "we could not see it" rather than "the
  // verbs did not emit it". They run BEFORE any answer is printed, because an instrument that
  // cannot see a channel has not earned the right to report a finding on that channel.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- instrument health ----------------------------------------------------------");

  // (a) one page load per run label — index-based attribution is only monotonic within a load.
  const multiLoad = ["real", "selftest", "synthetic"].filter((r) => (eventsOf(r).loads.length || 1) > 1);
  const loadsOk = mustPass(
    "each run label saw exactly one page load (index attribution is per-load)",
    multiLoad.length === 0,
    multiLoad.length ? `re-loaded: ${multiLoad.join(", ")}` : "real, selftest, synthetic",
    "a run label was loaded more than once — event indices restarted, so phase attribution is invalid",
  );

  // (b) every input verb returned without error. An errored verb yields an EMPTY phase, and an empty
  //     phase classifies as "no keystrokes at all" / "no travel" — the two most damaging findings in
  //     this ticket, manufactured by a verb that never ran.
  const erroredPhases = realEntries.filter((p) => p.isError);
  const verbsOk = mustPass(
    "every verb in the real run returned without error",
    erroredPhases.length === 0,
    erroredPhases.length ? erroredPhases.map((p) => p.label).join(", ") : `${realEntries.length} phase(s)`,
    `an input verb errored (${erroredPhases.map((p) => p.label).join(", ")}) — its phase measures nothing, not "the verb emits nothing"`,
  );

  // (c) every phase drained before the next verb started, or its events leak into the next slice.
  const unsettled = realEntries.filter((p) => p.settleError);
  const settleOk = mustPass(
    "every phase reached quiescence before the next verb",
    unsettled.length === 0,
    unsettled.length ? unsettled.map((p) => `${p.label}: ${p.settleError}`).join(" | ") : `${realEntries.length} phase(s)`,
    "a phase never quiesced — events may be attributed to the wrong verb",
  );

  // (d) the capture path can see every event type at all.
  const capturedTypes = new Set(SELF.map((e) => e.type));
  const blindTo = EVENT_TYPES.filter((t) => !capturedTypes.has(t));
  const selfComplete = selfRun.marks.includes("selftest:done") && selfRun.errors.length === 0;
  const selfTestOk = mustPass(
    "the capture self-test observed every event type this instrument claims to record",
    blindTo.length === 0 && selfComplete,
    blindTo.length
      ? `BLIND TO: ${blindTo.join(", ")}${selfRun.errors.length ? ` | page errors: ${selfRun.errors.join("; ")}` : ""}`
      : selfComplete
        ? `${capturedTypes.size}/${EVENT_TYPES.length} types`
        : `self-test did not run to completion (marks: ${selfRun.marks.join(",") || "none"}; errors: ${selfRun.errors.join("; ") || "none"})`,
    `the capture path could not be proven for: ${blindTo.join(", ") || "(self-test incomplete)"} — a zero on those types is unreadable`,
  );
  record.capture = {
    dispatchedTypes: EVENT_TYPES,
    observedTypes: [...capturedTypes],
    blindTo,
    marks: selfRun.marks,
    errors: selfRun.errors,
    complete: selfComplete,
    proven: selfTestOk,
  };
  const canSee = (...types) => types.every((t) => capturedTypes.has(t));

  // (e) the control ran to completion in its expected shape. Its zeros are load-bearing (mouseDowns
  //     === 0 is a discriminator), and a control that threw partway produces those same zeros for
  //     the opposite reason — "we never got there" reads identically to "el.click() emits no
  //     mousedown", and the separation table would call it a pass.
  const controlExpected = {
    keyEvents: 3 * (TEXT.length + PRESS_KEY.length),
    inputEvents: 1,
    changes: 2,
    clicks: 1,
    mouseDowns: 0,
  };
  const controlActual = {
    keyEvents: countOf(S, "keydown", "keypress", "keyup"),
    inputEvents: countOf(S, "input"),
    changes: countOf(S, "change"),
    clicks: countOf(S, "click"),
    mouseDowns: countOf(S, "mousedown"),
  };
  const controlMismatch = Object.keys(controlExpected).filter((k) => controlActual[k] !== controlExpected[k]);
  const controlDone = synthRun.marks.includes("control:done");
  const controlRan = S.length > 0;
  const controlShapeOk = mustPass(
    "the negative control ran to completion in its expected shape",
    controlRan && controlDone && synthRun.errors.length === 0 && controlMismatch.length === 0,
    controlMismatch.length
      ? controlMismatch.map((k) => `${k}=${controlActual[k]} (expected ${controlExpected[k]})`).join(", ")
      : controlDone
        ? `${S.length} event(s), all legs reported done`
        : `did not report completion (marks: ${synthRun.marks.join(",") || "none"}; errors: ${synthRun.errors.join("; ") || "none"})`,
    "the negative control did not run to completion — its zeros cannot be read as discriminators",
  );
  record.control = { expected: controlExpected, actual: controlActual, mismatch: controlMismatch, marks: synthRun.marks, errors: synthRun.errors };

  // (f) the instrument can observe isTrusted === false at all. Without at least one untrusted event
  //     somewhere, "every real event is trusted" is equally explained by an isTrusted read stuck at
  //     true, and Q1 would be answering for the instrument rather than for the verbs.
  const trustedInControl = S.filter((e) => e.isTrusted);
  const untrustedObservable = S.some((e) => !e.isTrusted);
  check("the instrument observes isTrusted === false when it should (in the control)", untrustedObservable, `${S.length - trustedInControl.length}/${S.length} control events untrusted`);
  if (!untrustedObservable && S.length) fail("no untrusted event was observed anywhere — the isTrusted read cannot be shown to work");
  if (trustedInControl.length) {
    note(`the negative control carries ${trustedInControl.length} TRUSTED event(s) (${[...new Set(trustedInControl.map((e) => e.type))].join(", ")}). The control is supposed to be 100% page-script-generated; every trusted event in it erodes the trustedFraction axis and must be explained before that axis is quoted.`);
  }

  // (g) the real run's event log arrived COMPLETE. Every rule in the acceptability section is a
  //     threshold against a count — `keydown >= chars`, `enterKeydown >= 1`, mousedown+mouseup+click
  //     present, the pointer approach spans far enough — so a batch that never reached the server is silently
  //     converted into a NOT-ACCEPTABLE verdict about the gateway, and through the type-body rule
  //     into "#110 SHOULD OPEN": a ticket opened on events that WERE emitted and then lost in
  //     transit. A "counts are lower bounds" note printed above the tables does not stop that,
  //     because nothing downstream reads the note. A missing index is positive proof of loss (the
  //     page numbers every event it records and advances its send cursor only on a successful POST),
  //     so this cannot fire on a healthy run. It is asserted for the REAL run only: the control's
  //     completeness is already covered by its exact-count shape assertion in (e) and the self-test's
  //     by the blind-type list in (d).
  //     KNOWN LIMIT: `missing` sees INTERIOR gaps only. A lost tail lowers the highest index along
  //     with the count, so a batch dropped at the very end of the run is invisible here — the drain
  //     navigation's unload beacon is what makes that case rare; this check does not cover it.
  const realLogComplete = mustPass(
    "the real run's event log arrived complete (no index gaps, nothing dropped at the page cap)",
    realRun.missing === 0 && realRun.dropped === 0,
    realRun.missing === 0 && realRun.dropped === 0
      ? `${R.length} event(s) over ${realRun.posts} POST(s)`
      : `${realRun.missing} index gap(s), ${realRun.dropped} dropped at the page cap`,
    `the real run's event log is incomplete (${realRun.missing} index gap(s), ${realRun.dropped} dropped) — its counts are lower bounds and no threshold may be applied to them`,
  );

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The negative-control separation table. It runs BEFORE the four questions on purpose: an
  // instrument that cannot separate real input from obviously-fake input has not earned the right
  // to answer them, and printing a verdict first and disclaiming it afterwards is how a broken
  // instrument's output gets quoted out of context.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- negative control: can the instrument tell real input from page-script fakery? ---");
  const axisOf = (events) => {
    const kd = events.filter((e) => e.type === "keydown");
    const moves = events.filter((e) => e.type === "mousemove" || e.type === "pointermove");
    const inter = summarize(deltas(kd));
    const keyish = events.filter((e) => e.type === "keydown" || e.type === "keypress" || e.type === "keyup");
    return {
      totalEvents: events.length,
      trustedFraction: events.length ? 1 - events.filter((e) => !e.isTrusted).length / events.length : null,
      keyEvents: keyish.length,
      // The keyboard channel's ONLY honest discriminator. See the discriminator table below for why
      // the raw `keyEvents` count cannot be one: page script can emit any number of KeyboardEvents
      // it likes, but `new KeyboardEvent(...)` is isTrusted === false and no page-script control can
      // forge a trusted one — that bit comes from the user agent or it does not come at all.
      trustedKeyEvents: keyish.filter((e) => e.isTrusted).length,
      pointerMoves: moves.length,
      mouseDowns: countOf(events, "mousedown"),
      beforeinput: countOf(events, "beforeinput"),
      inputEvents: countOf(events, "input"),
      medianKeyIntervalMs: inter ? inter.median : null,
    };
  };
  const axesReal = axisOf(R);
  const axesSynth = axisOf(S);
  /**
   * DISCRIMINATORS are the axes that carry behavioral meaning. `totalEvents` and
   * `medianKeyIntervalMs` deliberately are NOT discriminators: the two runs drive slightly different
   * amounts of work and the interval statistic is undefined whenever a run has no keystrokes, so
   * letting either "separate" the runs would make this acceptance criterion pass vacuously — the
   * exact failure mode #109 warns about. Each discriminator states the magnitude that counts.
   *
   * A `no` in this column is NOT evidence that the instrument is blind. Blindness is ruled out by
   * the capture self-test above, which is what actually gates each answer; this table only asks
   * whether real and fake are distinguishable at all.
   *
   * WHY THE RAW `keyEvents` COUNT IS NOT A DISCRIMINATOR (it used to be, and it could never fire).
   * The control emits 3 KeyboardEvents per character of TEXT plus 3 per character of PRESS_KEY —
   * 3 × (len + 1). A hypothetical per-character real path emits 3 × len for the fill plus 3 for the
   * press_key — 3 × len + 3. Those are the SAME NUMBER. Against any threshold at all the axis reads
   * `no`, in both the healthy and the broken case, so it could never demonstrate that this
   * instrument can tell a real keystroke from a dispatched one — it only ever rode along while the
   * mouse and trust axes did the separating. It stays in the table as CONTEXT so the collision is
   * visible, and the keyboard channel is discriminated on `trustedKeyEvents` instead: a property of
   * the keystrokes themselves that page script structurally cannot fake, rather than a count that
   * happens to collide.
   */
  const discriminators = [
    { key: "trustedFraction", why: "UA-generated vs page-script-generated", separates: (a, b) => a !== null && b !== null && Math.abs(a - b) >= 0.5 },
    { key: "pointerMoves", why: "a real pointer path vs none at all", separates: (a, b) => (a === 0) !== (b === 0) },
    { key: "trustedKeyEvents", why: "KEYBOARD CHANNEL: UA-generated keystrokes vs dispatched KeyboardEvents", separates: (a, b) => (a === 0) !== (b === 0) },
    { key: "beforeinput", why: "real editing intent vs a bare dispatched input", separates: (a, b) => (a === 0) !== (b === 0) },
    { key: "mouseDowns", why: "a real button press vs el.click()", separates: (a, b) => (a === 0) !== (b === 0) },
  ];
  /** Context-only axes carry their OWN reason for being context-only. A bare "not a discriminator"
   *  next to a number as suggestive as `keyEvents` invites the next reader to promote it back into
   *  the discriminator list without re-deriving the arithmetic that says it cannot work. */
  const contextWhy = {
    totalEvents: "context only — the two runs drive different amounts of work",
    keyEvents: "context only — raw counts COLLIDE (control 3*(len+1) vs per-character real 3*len+3); use trustedKeyEvents",
    inputEvents: "context only — one dispatched input is indistinguishable from one real input BY COUNT",
    medianKeyIntervalMs: "context only — undefined whenever either run has no keystrokes",
  };
  const rows = Object.keys(axesReal).map((k) => {
    const d = discriminators.find((x) => x.key === k);
    return {
      axis: k,
      real: r3(axesReal[k]) ?? axesReal[k],
      synthetic: r3(axesSynth[k]) ?? axesSynth[k],
      discriminator: Boolean(d),
      separates: d ? d.separates(axesReal[k], axesSynth[k]) : null,
      why: d?.why ?? contextWhy[k] ?? "context only — not a discriminator",
    };
  });
  console.log(`  ${"axis".padEnd(22)} ${"real".padStart(10)} ${"synthetic".padStart(10)}  ${"separates".padEnd(11)} note`);
  for (const row of rows) {
    console.log(
      `  ${row.axis.padEnd(22)} ${String(row.real).padStart(10)} ${String(row.synthetic).padStart(10)}  ${(row.discriminator ? (row.separates ? "YES" : "no") : "-").padEnd(11)} ${row.why}`,
    );
  }
  const separating = rows.filter((row) => row.discriminator && row.separates).map((row) => row.axis);

  /**
   * Say OUT LOUD, in the report and not only in a variable, whether the control validated the
   * KEYBOARD channel specifically. The mouse and trust axes separate on almost any working run, so
   * "the negative control separated" is a sentence that will get quoted as blanket validation of
   * every answer below it — including the keystroke answers, which are the ones this epic is
   * actually about. It is only true of the keyboard channel when a keystroke property the control
   * cannot forge came out different, and the trustedness of the key events themselves is that
   * property.
   */
  const keyboardChannelValidated = separating.includes("trustedKeyEvents");
  console.log("");
  console.log(`  KEYBOARD CHANNEL validated by this control: ${keyboardChannelValidated ? "YES" : "NO"}`);
  if (keyboardChannelValidated) {
    console.log(`    real emitted ${axesReal.trustedKeyEvents} trusted key event(s); the page-script control emitted ${axesSynth.trustedKeyEvents}.`);
    console.log("    A dispatched KeyboardEvent cannot carry isTrusted === true, so this separation is");
    console.log("    structural: it cannot be produced by a control that merely emitted fewer events.");
    // The count above is taken over the WHOLE real run, which includes browser_press_key and the
    // press("Enter") of the submit leg — both genuine keystroke paths. Saying only "the keyboard
    // channel is validated" invites the reader to carry that sentence onto the fill body, whose
    // keystroke count is the entire subject of this epic and is measured somewhere else. The NO
    // branch below spends eight lines preventing exactly that conflation; the YES branch has to
    // prevent it too, so the fill body's own trusted-keystroke count is printed right here.
    console.log(`    THOSE KEYSTROKES ARE NOT (necessarily) THE FILL BODY'S: the browser_type body phase`);
    console.log(`    contributed ${typePhase.filter((e) => e.isTrusted && typeof e.type === "string" && e.type.startsWith("key")).length} of them; the rest come from browser_press_key and the submit leg's`);
    console.log("    press(\"Enter\"). Read Q2 \"browser_type body\" for what the fill itself emitted.");
  } else {
    console.log(`    real emitted ${axesReal.trustedKeyEvents} trusted key event(s); the control emitted ${axesSynth.trustedKeyEvents}.`);
    console.log("    !! DO NOT QUOTE \"the control separated\" AS VALIDATION OF ANY KEYSTROKE MEASUREMENT.");
    console.log("    !! The axes that did separate (if any) are mouse-channel or whole-run-trust axes; the");
    console.log("    !! keyboard channel was not exercised on both sides of the comparison. Raw key-event");
    console.log("    !! COUNTS cannot substitute: the control emits 3*(len+1) and a per-character real path");
    console.log("    !! emits 3*len+3, which are the same number, so no count threshold can separate them.");
    console.log("    !! Note this is not, by itself, evidence the instrument is blind to keystrokes — the");
    console.log("    !! capture self-test above proves the channel is observable. It means the CONTROL did");
    console.log("    !! not demonstrate it, most likely because the real path emitted no trusted keystroke");
    console.log("    !! at all, which is itself the finding and must be read from Q2, not from this table.");
  }
  record.separation = { axesReal, axesSynth, rows, separatingAxes: separating, keyboardChannelValidated };

  const separationOk = mustPass(
    "the negative control is distinguishable from the real verb path",
    controlRan && separating.length > 0,
    separating.length ? `separating axes: ${separating.join(", ")}` : "no discriminating axis separates the two runs",
    "the real run and the page-script control are indistinguishable on every discriminating axis",
  );
  if (controlRan && separating.length === 0) {
    console.log("");
    console.log("  !! REFUSING TO REPORT A VERDICT ON THE REAL PATH.");
    console.log("  !! Two readings fit this outcome and the separation table alone cannot pick one:");
    console.log("  !!   (a) the instrument is broken in a way the self-test did not cover; or");
    console.log("  !!   (b) the real verb path is behaviorally identical to naive page-script synthesis.");
    console.log("  !! (b) would be the most serious finding this epic could produce, so it must be");
    console.log("  !! confirmed by hand rather than asserted by an instrument that just failed its own");
    console.log("  !! control. The numbers below are still printed — as RAW DATA, not as answers.");
  }

  /**
   * PER-QUESTION GATING. There is deliberately no single global "safe to answer" flag: with one, a
   * mouse-channel axis that separates on every working run would license the keyboard-channel
   * answers, and a blind keyboard capture would be published as "no keystrokes at all" — the
   * headline finding of this ticket, manufactured by an instrument that passed a control it was
   * never asked about. Each answer is licensed by the evidence for ITS OWN channel: the foundation
   * (attribution is valid, the control ran and separates), the capture self-test for the event types
   * that answer reads, and the health of the specific phase it summarizes.
   */
  const foundationWhy = [];
  if (!loadsOk) foundationWhy.push("a run was loaded more than once, so phase attribution is invalid");
  if (!controlShapeOk) foundationWhy.push("the negative control did not run to completion");
  if (!separationOk) foundationWhy.push("the instrument failed its negative control");
  // An incomplete real log closes every gate for the reason spelled out at health check (g): each
  // rule below is a threshold against a count, and a lower bound tested against a threshold produces
  // a verdict about the gateway out of a loss in the instrument.
  if (!realLogComplete) foundationWhy.push(`the real event log is incomplete (${realRun.missing} index gap(s), ${realRun.dropped} dropped at the page cap), so every count below is a lower bound and no threshold can be applied to it`);
  const foundationOk = foundationWhy.length === 0;
  /** Build a gate: returns the answer text when licensed, and names the missing evidence when not. */
  const gateOn = (conds) => {
    const why = [...foundationWhy, ...conds.filter((c) => !c.ok).map((c) => c.why)];
    const ok = why.length === 0;
    return { ok, why, verdict: (text) => (ok ? text : `UNVERIFIED — ${why.join("; ")}`) };
  };
  const phaseHealth = (entry, label) => [
    { ok: Boolean(entry), why: `the ${label} phase was never recorded` },
    { ok: !entry?.isError, why: `${label} returned an error, so its phase measures nothing` },
    { ok: !entry?.settleError, why: `the ${label} phase never quiesced, so its events may belong to another verb` },
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Q1..Q4 — the supporting numbers, and the answers they license.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- Q1  trustedness ------------------------------------------------------------");
  const untrusted = R.filter((e) => !e.isTrusted);
  const q1Gate = gateOn([
    { ok: untrustedObservable, why: "no untrusted event was observed anywhere, so a stuck-true isTrusted read is not excluded" },
    { ok: verbsOk && settleOk, why: "a verb errored or a phase never quiesced, so this is not the whole run" },
    { ok: R.length > 0, why: "the real run captured no events" },
  ]);
  /**
   * PER-VERB and PER-TARGET-TYPE, because the whole-run fraction cannot answer this question.
   * `fill()` branches on input type — a text field is completed over CDP Input.insertText (trusted)
   * and a date/range/color field by assigning `.value` and dispatching Events (untrusted) — so a
   * single aggregate over a run that is mostly mouse events can sit at 0.95 trusted and read as a
   * pass while an entire verb×target combination is 100% untrusted. The whole-run line below is
   * retained as context; the verdict reads the breakdown.
   */
  const verbGates = new Map(
    verbEntries.map((entry) => [
      entry,
      gateOn([
        ...phaseHealth(entry, entry.label),
        { ok: untrustedObservable, why: "no untrusted event was observed anywhere, so a stuck-true isTrusted read is not excluded" },
      ]),
    ]),
  );
  const perVerb = verbEntries.map((entry) => {
    const bad = entry.events.filter((e) => !e.isTrusted);
    return {
      verb: entry.verb,
      targetKind: entry.targetKind,
      label: entry.label,
      events: entry.events.length,
      untrusted: bad.length,
      untrustedTypes: [...new Set(bad.map((e) => e.type))],
      trustedFraction: entry.events.length ? r3(1 - bad.length / entry.events.length) : null,
      reported: verbGates.get(entry).ok,
      unverifiedBecause: verbGates.get(entry).why,
    };
  });
  /**
   * The same events regrouped by the ELEMENT they were delivered to. This view exists because phase
   * attribution is temporal and focus is not: blurring the text field to reach the date field fires
   * a trusted `blur` (and a `change` if the value moved) that the PAGE records after the next
   * phase's start index, so it lands in the date phase's slice while being emitted by the text
   * field. Grouping by target is immune to that — an untrusted event on `#realism-date` is an
   * untrusted event on the date input no matter which phase boundary it fell inside.
   */
  const TARGET_KINDS = {
    "#realism-field": "input[type=text]",
    "#realism-date": "input[type=date]",
    "#realism-range": "input[type=range]",
    "#realism-button": "button",
    "#realism-choice": "select",
  };
  const verbOnlyEvents = verbEntries.flatMap((p) => p.events);
  const byTarget = new Map();
  for (const e of verbOnlyEvents) {
    const kind = TARGET_KINDS[e.target] ?? `${e.target} (unmapped)`;
    const b = byTarget.get(kind) ?? { kind, events: 0, untrusted: 0, types: new Set() };
    b.events++;
    if (!e.isTrusted) {
      b.untrusted++;
      b.types.add(e.type);
    }
    byTarget.set(kind, b);
  }
  const perTarget = [...byTarget.values()].map((b) => ({
    targetKind: b.kind,
    events: b.events,
    untrusted: b.untrusted,
    untrustedTypes: [...b.types],
    trustedFraction: b.events ? r3(1 - b.untrusted / b.events) : null,
  }));
  const verbOnlyUntrusted = verbOnlyEvents.filter((e) => !e.isTrusted);
  const q1 = {
    // `answer` reads the VERB-ONLY population, matching the printed verdict and the acceptability
    // rule. The whole-run figure stays under `wholeRunAnswer` so it is available as context and can
    // never be mistaken for the finding — a JSON consumer that grabbed `answer` when it was the
    // whole-run number would be quoting a fraction diluted by navigate, snapshot and teardown.
    answer: verbOnlyUntrusted.length === 0 ? "yes — every event an input verb generated carries isTrusted === true" : `no — ${verbOnlyUntrusted.length} of ${verbOnlyEvents.length} verb-generated events carry isTrusted === false`,
    wholeRunAnswer: untrusted.length === 0 ? "yes — every event in the whole run carries isTrusted === true" : `no — ${untrusted.length} of ${R.length} whole-run events carry isTrusted === false`,
    totalEvents: R.length,
    untrustedCount: untrusted.length,
    trustedFraction: R.length ? r3(1 - untrusted.length / R.length) : null,
    untrustedTypes: [...new Set(untrusted.map((e) => e.type))],
    // The verb-only aggregate is the one the acceptability rule reads: navigate, snapshot and the
    // teardown tail are the instrument's own traffic and no consumer verb produced them.
    verbOnly: {
      events: verbOnlyEvents.length,
      untrusted: verbOnlyUntrusted.length,
      untrustedTypes: [...new Set(verbOnlyUntrusted.map((e) => e.type))],
      trustedFraction: verbOnlyEvents.length ? r3(1 - verbOnlyUntrusted.length / verbOnlyEvents.length) : null,
    },
    perVerb,
    perTarget,
    // `answer` is the raw computation and is always present; `reported` says whether the evidence
    // licenses quoting it, and `unverifiedBecause` names what is missing. A JSON consumer that reads
    // `answer` without `reported` is reading a number, not a finding.
    reported: q1Gate.ok,
    unverifiedBecause: q1Gate.why,
  };
  record.questions.Q1 = q1;
  console.log(`  whole run (context — includes navigate, snapshot and the teardown tail):`);
  console.log(`    events=${R.length}  untrusted=${untrusted.length}  trustedFraction=${q1.trustedFraction}${untrusted.length ? `  types=${q1.untrustedTypes.join(", ")}` : ""}`);
  console.log(`  verb-generated events only (what the verdict reads):`);
  console.log(`    events=${q1.verbOnly.events}  untrusted=${q1.verbOnly.untrusted}  trustedFraction=${q1.verbOnly.trustedFraction}${q1.verbOnly.untrusted ? `  types=${q1.verbOnly.untrustedTypes.join(", ")}` : ""}`);
  console.log("");
  console.log(`  per VERB × TARGET TYPE:`);
  console.log(`    ${"verb".padEnd(22)} ${"target".padEnd(26)} ${"events".padStart(7)} ${"untrusted".padStart(10)}  reading`);
  // Indexed against verbEntries rather than looked up from the row: perVerb rows are plain data
  // headed for the JSON record and must not carry the gate closure, and indexOf() on a row object
  // would silently pick the wrong gate the day two phases produce identical rows.
  perVerb.forEach((v, i) => {
    const reading = v.events === 0
      ? "NO EVENTS AT ALL — the verb reported success and the page observed nothing"
      : v.untrusted === 0
        ? "all trusted"
        : `UNTRUSTED: ${v.untrustedTypes.join(", ")}`;
    console.log(`    ${String(v.verb).padEnd(22)} ${String(v.targetKind).padEnd(26)} ${String(v.events).padStart(7)} ${String(v.untrusted).padStart(10)}  ${verbGates.get(verbEntries[i]).verdict(reading)}`);
  });
  console.log("");
  console.log(`  per TARGET ELEMENT (immune to phase-boundary attribution — see the comment in source):`);
  console.log(`    ${"target".padEnd(26)} ${"events".padStart(7)} ${"untrusted".padStart(10)}  untrusted types`);
  for (const t of perTarget) {
    console.log(`    ${String(t.targetKind).padEnd(26)} ${String(t.events).padStart(7)} ${String(t.untrusted).padStart(10)}  ${t.untrustedTypes.join(", ") || "-"}`);
  }
  console.log("");
  console.log(`  isTrusted === true on every verb-generated event?  ${q1Gate.verdict(verbOnlyUntrusted.length === 0 ? "YES" : `NO — ${verbOnlyUntrusted.length}/${verbOnlyEvents.length} untrusted`)}`);

  console.log("");
  console.log("--- Q2  sequence completeness ---------------------------------------------------");
  const perChar = {
    chars: TEXT.length,
    keydown: countOf(typePhase, "keydown"),
    keypress: countOf(typePhase, "keypress"),
    keyup: countOf(typePhase, "keyup"),
    beforeinput: countOf(typePhase, "beforeinput"),
    input: countOf(typePhase, "input"),
    change: countOf(typePhase, "change"),
    finalValueLength: [...typePhase].reverse().find((e) => typeof e.valueLength === "number")?.valueLength ?? null,
  };
  // Three outcomes, not two. "the phase captured nothing at all" is a DIFFERENT measurement from
  // "one value-set and no keystrokes", and collapsing them lets an empty phase print the most
  // damaging answer in the ticket. With the capture self-test green and the verb reporting success,
  // an empty phase is a real (and severe) finding — but it is stated as itself.
  const typeEmpty = typePhase.length === 0;
  const perCharacter = perChar.keydown >= TEXT.length && perChar.keyup >= TEXT.length && perChar.input >= TEXT.length;
  const singleShot = !typeEmpty && perChar.input <= 1 && perChar.keydown === 0;
  const typeShape = typeEmpty
    ? "NO EVENTS AT ALL — the verb reported success and the page observed nothing"
    : perCharacter
      ? "PER-CHARACTER keystroke sequence"
      : singleShot
        ? "SINGLE value-set (no keystrokes at all)"
        : "PARTIAL — neither a full per-character sequence nor a lone value-set";
  const typeGate = gateOn([
    ...phaseHealth(typeEntry, "browser_type"),
    { ok: canSee("keydown", "keypress", "keyup"), why: "the self-test could not prove the keystroke channel is observable" },
    { ok: canSee("beforeinput", "input"), why: "the self-test could not prove the editing channel is observable" },
  ]);
  console.log(`  browser_type (text field, submit:false) over ${TEXT.length} character(s):`);
  console.log(`    keydown=${perChar.keydown}  keypress=${perChar.keypress}  keyup=${perChar.keyup}  beforeinput=${perChar.beforeinput}  input=${perChar.input}  change=${perChar.change}  finalValueLen=${perChar.finalValueLength}`);
  console.log(`    shape: ${typeGate.verdict(typeShape)}`);

  /**
   * The submit:true leg, split into its two mechanisms and reported separately rather than summed.
   * `BrowserCore.type(target, text, {submit})` is `loc.fill(text)` followed, when submit is set, by
   * `loc.press("Enter")` — a value-completion path and a genuine keystroke path inside ONE verb
   * call. Summing them produces "browser_type emitted 3 key events", which is true and useless: it
   * hides that the 13-character body emitted none of them and the single Enter emitted all three.
   * Every consumer that drives a search box takes exactly this leg, so the split is the finding.
   */
  const enterKeys = submitPhase.filter((e) => typeof e.type === "string" && e.type.startsWith("key") && e.key === "Enter");
  const submitShape = {
    events: submitPhase.length,
    order: submitPhase.map((e) => e.type),
    // "key events that are not the Enter" — i.e. what the FILL BODY emitted on its own.
    bodyKeyEvents: countOf(submitPhase, "keydown", "keypress", "keyup") - enterKeys.length,
    enterKeyEvents: enterKeys.length,
    enterKeydown: enterKeys.filter((e) => e.type === "keydown").length,
    enterKeyup: enterKeys.filter((e) => e.type === "keyup").length,
    // `every()` on an empty array is true, so an Enter that produced nothing must read n/a here and
    // not "all trusted" — a vacuous pass on the one keystroke this whole leg exists to measure.
    enterAllTrusted: enterKeys.length ? enterKeys.every((e) => e.isTrusted) : null,
    beforeinput: countOf(submitPhase, "beforeinput"),
    input: countOf(submitPhase, "input"),
    change: countOf(submitPhase, "change"),
    chars: TEXT.length,
  };
  const submitGate = gateOn([
    ...phaseHealth(submitEntry, "browser_type (submit:true)"),
    { ok: canSee("keydown", "keypress", "keyup"), why: "the self-test could not prove the keystroke channel is observable" },
  ]);
  console.log(`  browser_type (text field, submit:true) — fill body + press("Enter"):`);
  console.log(`    fill body key events=${submitShape.bodyKeyEvents} over ${TEXT.length} character(s)   Enter key events=${submitShape.enterKeyEvents} (keydown=${submitShape.enterKeydown} keyup=${submitShape.enterKeyup} allTrusted=${submitShape.enterAllTrusted ?? "n/a — no Enter events"})`);
  console.log(`    order: ${submitShape.order.join(" -> ") || "(none)"}`);
  const submitReading = submitPhase.length === 0
    ? "NO EVENTS AT ALL — the verb reported success and the page observed nothing"
    : `the fill body emitted ${submitShape.bodyKeyEvents} keystroke event(s) for ${TEXT.length} character(s); the submit key emitted ${submitShape.enterKeyEvents}`;
  console.log(`    reading: ${submitGate.verdict(submitReading)}`);

  /**
   * The SET-VALUE branch legs. Each is filtered to events whose target IS the element under test:
   * moving focus off the previously-typed field fires a trusted blur/change that the page records
   * after this phase's start index, and counting that as part of what the date field emitted would
   * dilute exactly the untrusted fraction this leg exists to expose.
   */
  const setValueLegs = [
    { entry: dateEntry, events: datePhase, kind: "input[type=date]", targetId: "#realism-date", value: DATE_VALUE },
    { entry: rangeEntry, events: rangePhase, kind: "input[type=range]", targetId: "#realism-range", value: RANGE_VALUE },
  ];
  const setValueGates = setValueLegs.map((leg) =>
    gateOn([
      ...phaseHealth(leg.entry, `browser_type (${leg.kind})`),
      { ok: canSee("input", "change"), why: "the self-test could not prove the input/change channel is observable" },
      { ok: untrustedObservable, why: "no untrusted event was observed anywhere, so a stuck-true isTrusted read is not excluded" },
    ]),
  );
  const setValueShapes = setValueLegs.map((leg, i) => {
    const own = leg.events.filter((e) => e.target === leg.targetId);
    const bad = own.filter((e) => !e.isTrusted);
    return {
      targetKind: leg.kind,
      value: leg.value,
      eventsOnTarget: own.length,
      eventsInPhase: leg.events.length,
      order: own.map((e) => e.type),
      keyEvents: countOf(own, "keydown", "keypress", "keyup"),
      beforeinput: countOf(own, "beforeinput"),
      input: countOf(own, "input"),
      change: countOf(own, "change"),
      untrusted: bad.length,
      untrustedTypes: [...new Set(bad.map((e) => e.type))],
      reported: setValueGates[i].ok,
      unverifiedBecause: setValueGates[i].why,
    };
  });
  setValueShapes.forEach((s, i) => {
    console.log(`  browser_type (${s.targetKind}, value ${JSON.stringify(s.value)}) — the driver's set-value branch:`);
    console.log(`    on-target events=${s.eventsOnTarget} (phase total ${s.eventsInPhase})  keyEvents=${s.keyEvents} beforeinput=${s.beforeinput} input=${s.input} change=${s.change} untrusted=${s.untrusted}`);
    console.log(`    order: ${s.order.join(" -> ") || "(none)"}`);
    const reading = s.eventsOnTarget === 0
      ? "NO EVENTS AT ALL on this element — the verb reported success and the page observed nothing"
      : s.untrusted > 0
        ? `UNTRUSTED (${s.untrustedTypes.join(", ")}) — value assigned directly and Events dispatched from page context`
        : "all events on this element are trusted — the driver did NOT take the dispatch path here";
    console.log(`    reading: ${setValueGates[i].verdict(reading)}`);
  });

  const clickGates = clickEntries.map((entry) =>
    gateOn([
      ...phaseHealth(entry, entry.label),
      { ok: canSee("mousedown", "mouseup", "click"), why: "the self-test could not prove the mouse-button channel is observable" },
      { ok: canSee("mousemove", "pointermove"), why: "the self-test could not prove the pointer-move channel is observable" },
    ]),
  );
  const clickShapes = clickPhases.map((events, idx) => {
    const order = events.map((e) => e.type);
    const firstDown = order.indexOf("mousedown");
    const moves = events.filter((e) => e.type === "mousemove" || e.type === "pointermove");
    const movesBeforeDown = firstDown === -1 ? moves.length : events.slice(0, firstDown).filter((e) => e.type === "mousemove" || e.type === "pointermove").length;
    return {
      index: idx,
      label: clickEntries[idx].label,
      empty: events.length === 0,
      order,
      hasMouseDownUpClick: order.includes("mousedown") && order.includes("mouseup") && order.includes("click"),
      correctOrder: firstDown !== -1 && firstDown < order.indexOf("mouseup") && order.indexOf("mouseup") < order.lastIndexOf("click"),
      moveCount: moves.length,
      movesBeforeFirstMouseDown: movesBeforeDown,
      reported: clickGates[idx].ok,
    };
  });
  // The counts and the raw order always print; only the READING they license is gated. Suppressing
  // the numbers along with the verdict is how an ungated re-reading of the same log later becomes
  // impossible — the whole point of the "still printed, as raw data" contract in the header.
  clickShapes.forEach((c, i) => {
    console.log(`  browser_click #${i + 1}: mousedown+mouseup+click=${c.hasMouseDownUpClick}  in-order=${c.correctOrder}  pointer moves=${c.moveCount} (before mousedown: ${c.movesBeforeFirstMouseDown})`);
    console.log(`    order: ${c.order.join(" -> ") || "(none)"}`);
    const reading = c.empty
      ? "NO EVENTS AT ALL — the click verb reported success and the page observed nothing"
      : c.hasMouseDownUpClick
        ? `full mousedown -> mouseup -> click${c.correctOrder ? " in order" : " but NOT in that order"}`
        : "INCOMPLETE mouse sequence";
    console.log(`    reading: ${clickGates[i].verdict(reading)}`);
  });

  // `every()` on an empty array is `true`, so an empty select phase would otherwise print
  // allTrusted=true — a vacuous pass on the axis most likely to surface an untrusted event.
  const selectShape = {
    order: selectPhase.map((e) => e.type),
    input: countOf(selectPhase, "input"),
    change: countOf(selectPhase, "change"),
    trusted: selectPhase.length ? selectPhase.every((e) => e.isTrusted) : null,
  };
  const selectGate = gateOn([
    ...phaseHealth(selectEntry, "browser_select_option"),
    { ok: canSee("input", "change"), why: "the self-test could not prove the input/change channel is observable" },
  ]);
  console.log(`  browser_select_option: ${selectShape.order.join(" -> ") || "(no events)"}  (input=${selectShape.input} change=${selectShape.change} allTrusted=${selectShape.trusted ?? "n/a — no events"})`);
  console.log(`    reading: ${selectGate.verdict(selectPhase.length ? `${selectShape.change >= 1 ? "fires change" : "fires NO change"}, ${selectShape.trusted ? "all events trusted" : "AT LEAST ONE UNTRUSTED EVENT"}` : "NO EVENTS AT ALL — the verb reported success and the page observed nothing")}`);

  const keyShape = { order: keyPhase.map((e) => e.type), keydown: countOf(keyPhase, "keydown"), keypress: countOf(keyPhase, "keypress"), input: countOf(keyPhase, "input"), keyup: countOf(keyPhase, "keyup") };
  const keyGate = gateOn([
    ...phaseHealth(keyEntry, "browser_press_key"),
    { ok: canSee("keydown", "keypress", "keyup"), why: "the self-test could not prove the keystroke channel is observable" },
  ]);
  console.log(`  browser_press_key(${JSON.stringify(PRESS_KEY)}): ${keyShape.order.join(" -> ") || "(no events)"}  (keydown=${keyShape.keydown} keypress=${keyShape.keypress} input=${keyShape.input} keyup=${keyShape.keyup})`);
  console.log(`    reading: ${keyGate.verdict(keyPhase.length ? (keyShape.keydown >= 1 && keyShape.keyup >= 1 ? "a real keydown/keyup pair" : "no complete keydown/keyup pair") : "NO EVENTS AT ALL — the verb reported success and the page observed nothing")}`);
  record.questions.Q2 = {
    type: { ...perChar, shape: typeEmpty ? "no-events" : perCharacter ? "per-character" : singleShot ? "single-value-set" : "partial", reported: typeGate.ok, unverifiedBecause: typeGate.why },
    typeSubmit: { ...submitShape, reported: submitGate.ok, unverifiedBecause: submitGate.why },
    setValueBranch: setValueShapes,
    clicks: clickShapes,
    select: { ...selectShape, reported: selectGate.ok, unverifiedBecause: selectGate.why },
    pressKey: { ...keyShape, reported: keyGate.ok, unverifiedBecause: keyGate.why },
  };

  console.log("");
  console.log("--- Q3  timing distribution -----------------------------------------------------");
  const keydowns = typePhase.filter((e) => e.type === "keydown");
  const interKeystroke = summarize(deltas(keydowns));
  const interTypeEvent = summarize(deltas(typePhase));
  const q3 = {
    basis: "Event.timeStamp (DOMHighResTimeStamp) for the whole series; the listener's performance.now() only if any event withheld a timeStamp",
    note: "Chrome coarsens high-resolution timestamps, but at a sub-millisecond granularity — far below the tens-of-ms scale a keystroke-cadence profiler reads — so the coarsening cannot manufacture or hide the effect being measured",
    interKeystrokeMs: interKeystroke,
    interTypeEventMs: interTypeEvent,
    keydownSamples: keydowns.length,
    reported: typeGate.ok,
    unverifiedBecause: typeGate.why,
  };
  record.questions.Q3 = q3;
  if (interKeystroke) {
    console.log(`  inter-KEYSTROKE (keydown->keydown), n=${interKeystroke.n}: min=${interKeystroke.min} median=${interKeystroke.median} max=${interKeystroke.max} mean=${interKeystroke.mean} stddev=${interKeystroke.stddev} variance=${interKeystroke.variance}`);
  } else {
    console.log(`  inter-KEYSTROKE: n/a — browser_type produced ${keydowns.length} keydown event(s) over ${TEXT.length} characters, so there is no interval distribution to report.`);
    console.log(`  ${typeGate.verdict("that absence IS the reading: a keystroke-cadence profiler has no cadence to profile here")}`);
  }
  if (interTypeEvent) {
    console.log(`  inter-EVENT within the type phase, n=${interTypeEvent.n}: min=${interTypeEvent.min} median=${interTypeEvent.median} max=${interTypeEvent.max} mean=${interTypeEvent.mean} stddev=${interTypeEvent.stddev} variance=${interTypeEvent.variance}`);
  }

  /**
   * DWELL — the WITHIN-gesture durations, which epic #94 names explicitly and which no inter-event
   * series can stand in for: the inter-event numbers above mix the time inside a gesture with the
   * time between gestures, and a vendor reads the two as separate features. Pointer dwell is
   * mousedown→mouseup on one press (a human press is tens of ms; a synthesized one is frequently
   * sub-millisecond or exactly 0). Key dwell is keydown→keyup on one key, matched on `code` so a
   * held modifier pairs with its own release. Both are already in the captured table — this only
   * surfaces them as a statistic, which is what makes them comparable across runs.
   */
  const pointerDwellSamples = clickPhases.flatMap((events) => pointerDwells(events));
  const keyDwellSamples = verbEntries.flatMap((p) => keyDwells(p.events));
  const pointerDwell = summarize(pointerDwellSamples);
  const keyDwell = summarize(keyDwellSamples);
  console.log(
    pointerDwell
      ? `  DWELL pointer (mousedown->mouseup), n=${pointerDwell.n}: min=${pointerDwell.min} median=${pointerDwell.median} max=${pointerDwell.max} mean=${pointerDwell.mean} stddev=${pointerDwell.stddev} variance=${pointerDwell.variance}`
      : "  DWELL pointer (mousedown->mouseup): n/a — no mousedown/mouseup pair was captured inside a click phase",
  );
  console.log(
    keyDwell
      ? `  DWELL key (keydown->keyup, matched on code), n=${keyDwell.n}: min=${keyDwell.min} median=${keyDwell.median} max=${keyDwell.max} mean=${keyDwell.mean} stddev=${keyDwell.stddev} variance=${keyDwell.variance}`
      : "  DWELL key (keydown->keyup): n/a — no keydown/keyup pair was captured in any verb phase",
  );
  record.questions.Q3.pointerDwellMs = pointerDwell;
  record.questions.Q3.keyDwellMs = keyDwell;
  // Dwell carries its OWN licence, separate from `reported` above: the cadence numbers come out of
  // the type phase and are gated on typeGate, while pointer dwell comes out of the click phases. A
  // single `reported` flag over both would let a healthy type phase license a dwell figure taken
  // from a click that errored.
  record.questions.Q3.dwellReported = clickGates.every((g) => g.ok);
  record.questions.Q3.dwellUnverifiedBecause = clickGates.flatMap((g) => g.why);
  // Context only, NOT a behavioral reading: this series spans the harness's own per-phase quiescence
  // waits, so its max/stddev are dominated by the instrument rather than by the verbs.
  const wholeRun = summarize(deltas(R));
  if (wholeRun) console.log(`  inter-EVENT across the whole real run (spans harness waits — context, not a cadence), n=${wholeRun.n}: min=${wholeRun.min} median=${wholeRun.median} max=${wholeRun.max} stddev=${wholeRun.stddev} variance=${wholeRun.variance}`);
  record.questions.Q3.wholeRunMs = wholeRun;

  console.log("");
  console.log("--- Q4  pointer trajectory ------------------------------------------------------");
  const pointerPaths = clickPhases.map((events, idx) => {
    const moves = events.filter((e) => e.type === "mousemove" || e.type === "pointermove");
    const down = events.find((e) => e.type === "mousedown");
    const path = moves.map((e) => ({ type: e.type, x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY, sx: e.screenX, sy: e.screenY, ts: r3(typeof e.eventTs === "number" ? e.eventTs : e.obsMs) }));
    const distinct = new Set(path.map((p) => `${p.x},${p.y}`)).size;
    // SPAN, NOT DISTINCTNESS. Counting distinct (x,y) pairs looked right and was wrong: the first
    // in-container run reported "travelled" for a path of
    //   (222.89999389648438, 124.37000274658203) -> (222, 124)
    // which is ONE logical position reported at two precisions — the pointer event carries
    // sub-pixel coordinates and the mouse event carries integers, so the pair differs by under a
    // pixel and satisfies "two distinct positions" while the pointer plainly never travelled. That
    // is the flattering-but-wrong reading this question exists to avoid, so the test is the widest
    // separation between any two points on the approach, against a threshold well above both
    // sub-pixel rounding and any plausible jitter.
    let spanPx = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < path.length; i++) {
      for (let j = i + 1; j < path.length; j++) {
        const d = Math.hypot(path[i].x - path[j].x, path[i].y - path[j].y);
        if (d > spanPx) spanPx = d;
      }
    }
    const travelled = spanPx >= MIN_POINTER_SPAN_PX;
    return {
      index: idx,
      label: clickEntries[idx].label,
      moveCount: moves.length,
      distinctPositions: distinct,
      spanPx: r3(spanPx),
      landedAt: down ? { x: down.clientX, y: down.clientY } : null,
      movesBeforeFirstMouseDown: clickShapes[idx]?.movesBeforeFirstMouseDown ?? null,
      // A teleport is "the pointer never travelled a meaningful distance on the way in" — a single
      // jump straight onto the target counts, so does no movement at all, and so does a cluster of
      // points inside one pixel. The mechanisms are named separately because they are different
      // things with the same verdict: no move events at all; moves that never left one position;
      // and moves whose whole span is sub-threshold.
      teleported: !travelled,
      kind: moves.length === 0
        ? "no-move-events"
        : travelled
          ? "travelled"
          : distinct <= 1
            ? "single-position"
            : "sub-pixel-cluster",
      path: path.slice(0, 200),
      reported: clickGates[idx].ok,
    };
  });
  record.questions.Q4 = {
    mode: headless ? "headless" : "headful (Xvfb when DISPLAY is set)",
    display: process.env.DISPLAY ?? null,
    clicks: pointerPaths,
  };
  console.log(`  reading taken in: ${headless ? "HEADLESS" : "HEADFUL"} mode (DISPLAY=${process.env.DISPLAY ?? "unset"}) — headful-under-Xvfb may differ from headless, so the mode is part of the reading.`);
  pointerPaths.forEach((p, i) => {
    const reading = p.kind === "no-move-events"
      ? "TELEPORT (no move events at all)"
      : p.kind === "single-position"
        ? "TELEPORT (moves, but never left one position)"
        : p.kind === "sub-pixel-cluster"
          ? `TELEPORT (moves span only ${p.spanPx}px — one position at two precisions, not travel)`
          : "travelled";
    console.log(`  click #${i + 1}: moves=${p.moveCount} spanPx=${p.spanPx} distinctPositions=${p.distinctPositions} beforeMouseDown=${p.movesBeforeFirstMouseDown} landedAt=${p.landedAt ? `(${p.landedAt.x},${p.landedAt.y})` : "n/a"} => ${clickGates[i].verdict(reading)}`);
    if (p.path.length) console.log(`    path: ${p.path.slice(0, 12).map((q) => `(${q.x},${q.y})`).join(" -> ")}${p.path.length > 12 ? ` ... +${p.path.length - 12}` : ""}`);
  });

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- summary ---------------------------------------------------------------------");
  // Reads the VERB-ONLY aggregate, not the whole run: navigate/snapshot/teardown events dilute the
  // fraction with traffic no consumer verb produced, and the whole-run number is one line above in
  // the Q1 section for anyone who wants it.
  const untrustedTargets = perTarget.filter((t) => t.untrusted > 0).map((t) => t.targetKind);
  console.log(`  Q1 trustedness  : ${q1Gate.verdict(verbOnlyUntrusted.length === 0 ? `all ${verbOnlyEvents.length} verb-generated events isTrusted=true` : `${verbOnlyUntrusted.length}/${verbOnlyEvents.length} verb-generated events isTrusted=FALSE on ${untrustedTargets.join(", ")}`)}`);
  console.log(`  Q2 type         : ${typeGate.verdict(`${record.questions.Q2.type.shape} (keydown=${perChar.keydown}/${TEXT.length}, input=${perChar.input})`)}`);
  console.log(`  Q2 type+submit  : ${submitGate.verdict(`fill body ${submitShape.bodyKeyEvents} key event(s), Enter ${submitShape.enterKeyEvents} key event(s)`)}`);
  setValueShapes.forEach((s, i) => {
    console.log(`  Q2 ${s.targetKind.padEnd(14)}: ${setValueGates[i].verdict(s.eventsOnTarget === 0 ? "no events at all" : s.untrusted > 0 ? `${s.untrusted}/${s.eventsOnTarget} UNTRUSTED (${s.untrustedTypes.join(", ")})` : "all trusted")}`);
  });
  clickShapes.forEach((c, i) => {
    console.log(`  Q2 click #${i + 1}     : ${clickGates[i].verdict(c.empty ? "no events at all" : c.hasMouseDownUpClick ? "mousedown+mouseup+click present" : "INCOMPLETE mouse sequence")}`);
  });
  console.log(`  Q3 timing       : ${typeGate.verdict(interKeystroke ? `inter-keystroke median=${interKeystroke.median}ms stddev=${interKeystroke.stddev}ms over n=${interKeystroke.n}` : "no keystroke intervals exist to distribute")}`);
  console.log(`  Q3 dwell        : pointer ${pointerDwell ? `median=${pointerDwell.median}ms over n=${pointerDwell.n}` : "n/a"}, key ${keyDwell ? `median=${keyDwell.median}ms over n=${keyDwell.n}` : "n/a"}`);
  // Compact per-click: an ungated click reads as its shape, a gated one as a bare UNVERIFIED. The
  // full reason is one section up and in the JSON record — repeating it once per click here would
  // bury the four summary lines this block exists to make scannable.
  console.log(`  Q4 trajectory   : ${pointerPaths.map((p, i) => (clickGates[i].ok ? `click#${i + 1} ${p.moveCount} move(s) [${p.kind}]` : `click#${i + 1} UNVERIFIED`)).join(", ") || "no clicks measured"} [${headless ? "headless" : "headful"}]`);
  if (!foundationOk) console.log(`  gating          : ${foundationWhy.join("; ")}`);
  console.log(`  evidence path   : ${useStdio ? "real stdio launcher + real policy/egress" : "IN-PROCESS FALLBACK (weaker evidence — see the transport section)"}`);

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // ACCEPTABILITY. Everything above is a measurement; this section is the judgement #109 owes, and
  // it is separated from the measurement on purpose. Numbers without a stated threshold get a
  // threshold supplied by whoever reads them, and every reader supplies a different one — which is
  // how a report that says "0 keydowns over 13 characters" ends up quoted as "fine, it worked".
  //
  // Every rule below is applied to the data, never to an expectation: each one can come out
  // ACCEPTABLE or NOT-ACCEPTABLE depending on what the run produced, and the thresholds live
  // literally next to the rules that read them so a future reader can disagree with a NUMBER rather
  // than with a verdict. A rule whose gate is closed produces UNVERIFIED and never a guess — which
  // is also what makes the negative-control gating bite: `gateOn` folds the foundation failures
  // (including "the control did not separate") into every gate, so a failed control turns every
  // judgement on this page into UNVERIFIED without a separate code path that could drift.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- acceptability against a behavioral detector ---------------------------------");
  console.log("  WHAT \"ACCEPTABLE\" MEANS HERE: not obviously synthetic on the axes THIS FIXTURE can");
  console.log("  see, measured against a local page with no detector on it. It is NOT a claim that a");
  console.log("  vendor would fail to flag the session: real vendors read features this instrument");
  console.log("  does not model at all (mouse-path curvature, focus/idle rhythm, cross-page history).");
  console.log("  An ACCEPTABLE here means \"no self-evident tell was measured\", never \"undetectable\".");

  /**
   * The thresholds, in one place, each with the human behavior it encodes. They are deliberately
   * WIDE: this instrument's job is to catch a self-evident tell (zero keystrokes, an untrusted
   * event, a teleporting pointer), not to adjudicate whether a real cadence is realistic enough. A
   * narrow band here would manufacture NOT-ACCEPTABLE verdicts on healthy input and burn the
   * credibility of the ones that matter.
   */
  const ACCEPT = {
    /** isTrusted is a boolean a detector reads directly off the event. There is no partial credit
     *  and no threshold to tune: one untrusted event on a verb-driven surface is the tell. */
    maxUntrustedVerbEvents: 0,
    /** A human typing N characters produces N keydowns and N keyups. Anything short of that leaves
     *  a keystroke-cadence model with fewer samples than the value length implies — itself a tell,
     *  and at zero it leaves the model with nothing at all. */
    keydownsPerChar: 1,
    /** Three intervals is the smallest thing that is a distribution rather than two numbers. */
    minKeystrokeIntervalSamples: 3,
    /** Human inter-keystroke medians cluster around 60-250ms. The accepted band is much wider so a
     *  genuinely fast typist is not failed, while a 0-1ms machine-gun burst still fails. */
    keystrokeMedianMsMin: 20,
    keystrokeMedianMsMax: 500,
    /** Zero spread across >= 3 samples is a metronome. No hand produces one. */
    minKeystrokeStddevMs: 1,
    /**
     * Pointer dwell — the time the button is HELD, mousedown→mouseup. Two thresholds, because one
     * cannot carry both readings, and the earlier single 1ms floor contradicted the behavior its own
     * comment named: a human press holds for tens of ms, but at a 1ms floor a 2ms press — which is
     * what two back-to-back `Input.dispatchMouseEvent` round-trips with no delay between them
     * produce — came out ACCEPTABLE. That reads as a pass on a button press three orders of
     * magnitude faster than any hand. It is masked today only because the cadence surface is
     * NOT-ACCEPTABLE and outranks it in the Q3 roll-up; the moment #110 lands and cadence goes
     * ACCEPTABLE, dwell BECOMES the Q3 verdict and the report would print "Q3 timing => ACCEPTABLE"
     * off that same 2ms. The cadence rule next to it is calibrated to human behavior ([20,500]ms);
     * dwell has to be too.
     *   instantPointerDwellMedianMs — below this the press and the release share a millisecond. It
     *     is the strongest form of the tell and is named separately so the reason string can say so.
     *   minPointerDwellMedianMs — the low edge of human press duration. Real presses cluster around
     *     60-120ms; 30ms is deliberately under the fastest plausible deliberate click so a quick
     *     hand is not failed, while anything in the single-digit-ms range cannot read ACCEPTABLE.
     */
    instantPointerDwellMedianMs: 1,
    minPointerDwellMedianMs: 30,
    /** "Travelled" = the widest separation between any two points on the approach is at least this
     *  many CSS pixels. Distance, not a count of distinct coordinate pairs: the first in-container
     *  run reported "travelled" off a 0.95px gap between a pointer event's sub-pixel coordinates
     *  and the mouse event's integer ones — one logical position at two precisions. 8px is far
     *  above sub-pixel rounding and any plausible jitter, and far below the tens-to-hundreds of
     *  pixels a pointer crossing a page would cover, so neither error is close to the line. */
    minPointerSpanPx: MIN_POINTER_SPAN_PX,
  };
  record.acceptability = { thresholds: ACCEPT, questions: {} };

  const ACCEPTABLE = "ACCEPTABLE";
  const NOT_ACCEPTABLE = "NOT-ACCEPTABLE";
  const INDETERMINATE = "INDETERMINATE";
  const UNVERIFIED = "UNVERIFIED";
  /** Apply a rule only where its gate licenses it. A closed gate is UNVERIFIED, never a guess. */
  const judged = (gate, fn) => (gate.ok ? fn() : { verdict: UNVERIFIED, reason: gate.why.join("; ") });
  /**
   * Roll several surface verdicts into one. NOT-ACCEPTABLE outranks UNVERIFIED deliberately: a tell
   * that was actually measured on one surface is not redeemed by another surface being unreadable,
   * and the opposite precedence would let a single gated phase launder a confirmed finding into
   * "we could not tell".
   */
  const worstOf = (parts) => {
    const order = [NOT_ACCEPTABLE, UNVERIFIED, INDETERMINATE, ACCEPTABLE];
    for (const v of order) {
      const hit = parts.filter((p) => p.verdict === v);
      if (hit.length) return { verdict: v, reason: hit.map((p) => `${p.name}: ${p.reason}`).join(" | ") };
    }
    return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "nothing was measured" };
  };

  // --- Q1 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // RULE: ACCEPTABLE iff no event produced by an input verb carries isTrusted === false.
  const jQ1 = judged(q1Gate, () => {
    if (verbOnlyEvents.length === 0) return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "no input verb produced an observable event, so there is no trustedness to judge" };
    if (verbOnlyUntrusted.length > ACCEPT.maxUntrustedVerbEvents) {
      const where = perTarget.filter((t) => t.untrusted > 0).map((t) => `${t.targetKind} x${t.untrusted} (${t.untrustedTypes.join("/")})`).join(", ");
      return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `${verbOnlyUntrusted.length}/${verbOnlyEvents.length} verb-generated events carry isTrusted === false, on ${where}` };
    }
    return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `all ${verbOnlyEvents.length} verb-generated events carry isTrusted === true, across ${perTarget.length} target type(s)` };
  });
  /**
   * Q1 decomposed per TARGET KIND, printed alongside the headline the way Q2's surfaces are. The
   * headline rule above stays the population rule — one untrusted verb-generated event anywhere is
   * the tell — but on this fixture it can only ever come out NOT-ACCEPTABLE or UNVERIFIED: the
   * driver's set-value branch and `selectOptions()` both END in dispatched (untrusted) input/change
   * events, so a HEALTHY run carries at least six of them by construction. A reader who quotes only
   * the one-line Q1 verdict therefore learns nothing about the surfaces a consumer actually drives —
   * the text field, the button, the pressed key — which may be entirely clean underneath that
   * guaranteed NOT-ACCEPTABLE. These parts are decomposition, not a second rule: they share q1Gate
   * and the same threshold, so their roll-up can never disagree with the headline. A kind appears
   * here only if at least one event landed on it, so there is no empty case to judge.
   */
  const q1Parts = perTarget.map((t) => ({
    id: `target:${t.targetKind}`,
    name: t.targetKind,
    ...judged(q1Gate, () =>
      t.untrusted > ACCEPT.maxUntrustedVerbEvents
        ? { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `${t.untrusted}/${t.events} events untrusted (${t.untrustedTypes.join(", ")})` }
        : { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `all ${t.events} events on this target are trusted` }),
  }));

  // --- Q2 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // RULES, per surface (Q2 overall is the worst of them):
  //   type body : ACCEPTABLE iff keydown >= chars AND keyup >= chars (a per-character sequence);
  //               INDETERMINATE if the phase captured nothing at all, which is a statement about
  //               the instrument and not about the fill.
  //   type+submit: ACCEPTABLE iff the Enter emitted a keydown AND a keyup AND both are trusted.
  //   set-value : ACCEPTABLE iff the element saw at least one event and none is untrusted.
  //   click     : ACCEPTABLE iff mousedown, mouseup and click all fired, in that order.
  //   select    : ACCEPTABLE iff a change fired and every event on the phase is trusted.
  //   press_key : ACCEPTABLE iff a keydown and a keyup fired and every key event is trusted.
  const keyPhaseKeys = keyPhase.filter((e) => typeof e.type === "string" && e.type.startsWith("key"));
  const q2Parts = [
    {
      // `id` is what the #110 decision below matches on. The display `name` is prose and will be
      // reworded; matching the decision's input by that string is the same fragility the phase
      // lookup abandoned when three phases started with "browser_type", and its failure here is
      // silent — a reworded label falls through to the INDETERMINATE default and the ticket
      // decision quietly becomes CANNOT DECIDE with the reason "the type body surface was not
      // judged", which is fail-safe and completely uninformative about why.
      id: "type-body",
      name: "browser_type body (text)",
      ...judged(typeGate, () => {
        // "the phase measured nothing" is not "the fill emitted no keystrokes", and only the second
        // of those is a finding about the gateway. Every sibling surface already makes this
        // distinction (the set-value legs and select both return INDETERMINATE on an empty phase)
        // and the printed Q2 reading for this very phase says "NO EVENTS AT ALL — the verb reported
        // success and the page observed nothing" — but the rule collapsed that into a plain
        // NOT-ACCEPTABLE "0 keydown / 0 keyup", which is then the sole input to "#110 SHOULD OPEN".
        // Nothing upstream catches the case: verbsOk/settleOk pass (the verb returned success), the
        // self-test is green, and a fixture edit that re-targets the field or a batch lost inside
        // one phase both land here. INDETERMINATE routes it to CANNOT DECIDE, which is what the
        // decision rule already says it wants: an unverified instrument must not close a ticket,
        // and it must not open one either.
        if (typeEmpty) return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "the type phase produced no events at all, so the shape of the fill body cannot be characterized — this is an instrument/fixture question first (the verb reported success and the page observed nothing), not a measurement of the keystroke path" };
        if (perChar.keydown >= TEXT.length * ACCEPT.keydownsPerChar && perChar.keyup >= TEXT.length * ACCEPT.keydownsPerChar) {
          return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `per-character sequence — keydown=${perChar.keydown} keyup=${perChar.keyup} over ${TEXT.length} character(s)` };
        }
        return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `${perChar.keydown} keydown / ${perChar.keyup} keyup over ${TEXT.length} character(s) (shape: ${record.questions.Q2.type.shape}) — a detector counting keystrokes against the value length sees the mismatch directly` };
      }),
    },
    {
      id: "type-submit-key",
      name: "browser_type submit key",
      ...judged(submitGate, () => {
        if (submitShape.enterKeyEvents === 0) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: "submit:true emitted no key event at all for the Enter" };
        if (submitShape.enterKeydown < 1 || submitShape.enterKeyup < 1) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `incomplete Enter keystroke — keydown=${submitShape.enterKeydown} keyup=${submitShape.enterKeyup}` };
        if (submitShape.enterAllTrusted !== true) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: "the Enter key events are not all isTrusted === true" };
        return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `a real keystroke — ${submitShape.enterKeyEvents} trusted Enter key event(s)` };
      }),
    },
    ...setValueShapes.map((s, i) => ({
      id: `set-value:${s.targetKind}`,
      name: `browser_type ${s.targetKind}`,
      ...judged(setValueGates[i], () => {
        if (s.eventsOnTarget === 0) return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "no event was observed on this element, so its branch cannot be characterized" };
        if (s.untrusted > ACCEPT.maxUntrustedVerbEvents) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `${s.untrusted}/${s.eventsOnTarget} events untrusted (${s.untrustedTypes.join(", ")}) — the set-value branch assigns .value and dispatches Events from page context` };
        return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `all ${s.eventsOnTarget} events on this element are trusted` };
      }),
    })),
    ...clickShapes.map((c, i) => ({
      id: `click:${i + 1}`,
      name: `browser_click #${i + 1}`,
      ...judged(clickGates[i], () => {
        if (c.empty) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: "the click produced no events at all" };
        if (!c.hasMouseDownUpClick) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `incomplete mouse sequence (${c.order.join(" -> ")})` };
        if (!c.correctOrder) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `mousedown/mouseup/click present but out of order (${c.order.join(" -> ")})` };
        return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: "mousedown -> mouseup -> click, in order" };
      }),
    })),
    {
      id: "select-option",
      name: "browser_select_option",
      ...judged(selectGate, () => {
        if (selectPhase.length === 0) return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "the select phase produced no events" };
        if (selectShape.trusted !== true) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `at least one untrusted event (${[...new Set(selectPhase.filter((e) => !e.isTrusted).map((e) => e.type))].join(", ")}) — selectOptions dispatches input/change from page context` };
        if (selectShape.change < 1) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: "no change event fired, which no user-driven selection omits" };
        return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `change fired and all ${selectPhase.length} events are trusted` };
      }),
    },
    {
      id: "press-key",
      name: "browser_press_key",
      ...judged(keyGate, () => {
        if (keyPhase.length === 0) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: "the press-key verb produced no events at all" };
        if (keyShape.keydown < 1 || keyShape.keyup < 1) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `no complete keydown/keyup pair (keydown=${keyShape.keydown} keyup=${keyShape.keyup})` };
        if (!keyPhaseKeys.every((e) => e.isTrusted)) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: "at least one key event is isTrusted === false" };
        return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `trusted keydown/keyup pair (${keyShape.order.join(" -> ")})` };
      }),
    },
  ];
  const jQ2 = worstOf(q2Parts);

  // --- Q3 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // RULES:
  //   cadence : ACCEPTABLE iff there are >= minKeystrokeIntervalSamples inter-keydown intervals AND
  //             the median falls inside [keystrokeMedianMsMin, keystrokeMedianMsMax] AND the stddev
  //             is >= minKeystrokeStddevMs. Zero intervals is NOT-ACCEPTABLE, not indeterminate:
  //             an empty cadence feature is exactly what a cadence model reads off automation.
  //   dwell   : ACCEPTABLE iff the pointer dwell median >= minPointerDwellMedianMs (the human band's
  //             low edge). A median under instantPointerDwellMedianMs is called out as the stronger
  //             form — pressed and released inside the same millisecond — but both are the same
  //             verdict: a press no hand could produce.
  const jQ3Cadence = judged(typeGate, () => {
    if (!interKeystroke) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `no inter-keystroke intervals exist (${keydowns.length} keydown over ${TEXT.length} characters) — a keystroke-cadence model gets an empty feature vector` };
    if (interKeystroke.n < ACCEPT.minKeystrokeIntervalSamples) return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: `only ${interKeystroke.n} interval(s); ${ACCEPT.minKeystrokeIntervalSamples} are needed before a distribution can be judged` };
    if (interKeystroke.median < ACCEPT.keystrokeMedianMsMin || interKeystroke.median > ACCEPT.keystrokeMedianMsMax) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `median ${interKeystroke.median}ms is outside the human band [${ACCEPT.keystrokeMedianMsMin}, ${ACCEPT.keystrokeMedianMsMax}]ms` };
    if (interKeystroke.stddev < ACCEPT.minKeystrokeStddevMs) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `stddev ${interKeystroke.stddev}ms is below ${ACCEPT.minKeystrokeStddevMs}ms — a metronome, not a hand` };
    return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `median ${interKeystroke.median}ms, stddev ${interKeystroke.stddev}ms over n=${interKeystroke.n}` };
  });
  const clicksGate = gateOn(clickGates.map((g, i) => ({ ok: g.ok, why: `click #${i + 1}: ${g.why.join("; ")}` })));
  const jQ3Dwell = judged(clicksGate, () => {
    if (!pointerDwell) return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "no mousedown/mouseup pair was captured, so there is no dwell to judge" };
    if (pointerDwell.median < ACCEPT.instantPointerDwellMedianMs) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `pointer dwell median ${pointerDwell.median}ms is below ${ACCEPT.instantPointerDwellMedianMs}ms — the button is pressed and released inside the same millisecond, which no hand does` };
    if (pointerDwell.median < ACCEPT.minPointerDwellMedianMs) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `pointer dwell median ${pointerDwell.median}ms is below the human press-duration floor of ${ACCEPT.minPointerDwellMedianMs}ms (real presses cluster around 60-120ms) — the down/up pair is two back-to-back dispatches, not a held button` };
    return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `pointer dwell median ${pointerDwell.median}ms over n=${pointerDwell.n}${keyDwell ? `; key dwell median ${keyDwell.median}ms over n=${keyDwell.n}` : ""}` };
  });
  /**
   * Kept as an array — and PRINTED below, the way Q2's surfaces are — because `worstOf` returns only
   * the parts sitting at the worst tier and drops the rest. With cadence NOT-ACCEPTABLE (the
   * expected outcome until #110 lands) and a pointer dwell of exactly 0ms — a STRONGER tell than the
   * cadence one — the rolled-up `jQ3.reason` would name the cadence alone, and the dwell judgement
   * would appear nowhere at all: not in the summary, not in `record.acceptability.questions.Q3`,
   * and not in `record.decision110`, which keeps the cadence part only. Q3's raw dwell NUMBERS are
   * recorded up in `record.questions.Q3`, but a number without its verdict is exactly what the
   * acceptability section exists to stop being published.
   */
  const q3Parts = [
    { id: "keystroke-cadence", name: "keystroke cadence", ...jQ3Cadence },
    { id: "pointer-dwell", name: "pointer dwell", ...jQ3Dwell },
  ];
  const jQ3 = worstOf(q3Parts);

  // --- Q4 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // RULE: ACCEPTABLE iff every measured click's approach spans >= minPointerSpanPx. Distance, not
  //       a count of distinct coordinate pairs — see the threshold's comment for the sub-pixel
  //       false-positive this replaced. No movement, one position, and a sub-pixel cluster are all
  //       teleports; only the reason string distinguishes them.
  const jQ4 = judged(clicksGate, () => {
    if (pointerPaths.length === 0) return { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "no click was measured" };
    const teleports = pointerPaths.filter((p) => p.teleported);
    if (teleports.length) return { verdict: NOT_ACCEPTABLE, reason: `${teleports.length}/${pointerPaths.length} click(s) teleported: ${teleports.map((p) => `#${p.index + 1} ${p.kind} (span ${p.spanPx}px over ${p.moveCount} move(s), threshold ${ACCEPT.minPointerSpanPx}px)`).join(", ")}` };
    return { verdict: ACCEPTABLE, reason: `all ${pointerPaths.length} click(s) travelled — min approach span ${Math.min(...pointerPaths.map((p) => p.spanPx))}px` };
  });

  const judgements = [
    { q: "Q1 trustedness", measured: verbOnlyEvents.length ? `${verbOnlyUntrusted.length}/${verbOnlyEvents.length} verb-generated events untrusted` : "no verb-generated events", ...jQ1 },
    { q: "Q2 completeness", measured: `type=${record.questions.Q2.type.shape}, submitEnter=${submitShape.enterKeyEvents} key event(s), clicks=${clickShapes.filter((c) => c.hasMouseDownUpClick).length}/${clickShapes.length} complete, select ${selectShape.change >= 1 ? "fires change" : "fires no change"}`, ...jQ2 },
    { q: "Q3 timing", measured: `${interKeystroke ? `inter-keystroke median=${interKeystroke.median}ms stddev=${interKeystroke.stddev}ms n=${interKeystroke.n}` : "no inter-keystroke intervals"}; ${pointerDwell ? `pointer dwell median=${pointerDwell.median}ms` : "no pointer dwell"}`, ...jQ3 },
    { q: "Q4 trajectory", measured: pointerPaths.length ? pointerPaths.map((p) => `click#${p.index + 1} ${p.moveCount} move(s)/${p.spanPx}px span`).join(", ") : "no clicks measured", ...jQ4 },
  ];
  console.log("");
  for (const j of judgements) {
    console.log(`  ${j.q.padEnd(17)}: ${j.measured}`);
    console.log(`  ${" ".repeat(17)}  => ${j.verdict} — ${j.reason}`);
  }
  // Every rolled-up question prints its parts. The roll-up itself is lossy by design (`worstOf`
  // keeps the worst tier and discards the rest), so without these blocks a surface that was judged
  // and came out at a better — or merely different — tier than its sibling has no output anywhere,
  // and the aggregate hides the per-case failure the reader needs.
  console.log("");
  console.log("  per-surface Q1 detail (the headline rule is the whole verb-generated population):");
  for (const p of q1Parts) console.log(`    ${p.name.padEnd(30)} ${p.verdict.padEnd(15)} ${p.reason}`);
  console.log("");
  console.log("  per-surface Q2 detail:");
  for (const p of q2Parts) console.log(`    ${p.name.padEnd(30)} ${p.verdict.padEnd(15)} ${p.reason}`);
  console.log("");
  console.log("  per-surface Q3 detail:");
  for (const p of q3Parts) console.log(`    ${p.name.padEnd(30)} ${p.verdict.padEnd(15)} ${p.reason}`);
  record.acceptability.questions = Object.fromEntries(judgements.map((j) => [j.q.split(" ")[0], { measured: j.measured, verdict: j.verdict, reason: j.reason }]));
  record.acceptability.q1Surfaces = q1Parts.map((p) => ({ id: p.id, surface: p.name, verdict: p.verdict, reason: p.reason }));
  record.acceptability.q2Surfaces = q2Parts.map((p) => ({ id: p.id, surface: p.name, verdict: p.verdict, reason: p.reason }));
  record.acceptability.q3Surfaces = q3Parts.map((p) => ({ id: p.id, surface: p.name, verdict: p.verdict, reason: p.reason }));

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // HAND-OFF TO #110 (the conditional timing model: per-character typing with human-plausible
  // inter-key delays, applied conditionally). #109's job is to hand that ticket a yes or a no with
  // the facts attached, so nobody has to re-derive the decision from the tables above.
  //
  // RULE:
  //   CLOSE AS NOT-NEEDED  iff the fill body ALREADY types per-character (Q2 type body ACCEPTABLE)
  //                            AND the resulting cadence is human-plausible (Q3 cadence ACCEPTABLE).
  //                            Both must hold: a per-character path with a 0ms metronome cadence is
  //                            exactly the thing #110 exists to fix.
  //   SHOULD OPEN          iff either of those is NOT-ACCEPTABLE — there is a concrete gap to close.
  //   CANNOT DECIDE        iff either is UNVERIFIED or INDETERMINATE. An unverified instrument must
  //                            not close a ticket, and it must not open one either.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The decision reads the two SURFACE verdicts it actually depends on, not the rolled-up Q2/Q3
  // verdicts: a NOT-ACCEPTABLE click trajectory would drag the whole of Q2 down and, through it,
  // open #110 — a timing-model ticket — for a mouse-path defect it has nothing to do with.
  // Matched on the stable `id`, never on the display label: `name` is prose, and a reword there
  // would silently drop this lookup onto its fallback and turn the ticket decision into CANNOT
  // DECIDE with a reason that describes nothing.
  const typeBody = q2Parts.find((p) => p.id === "type-body") ?? { verdict: INDETERMINATE, reason: "the type body surface was not judged (its acceptability part is missing — this is an instrument bug, not a measurement)" };
  const decidable = [typeBody, jQ3Cadence].every((v) => v.verdict === ACCEPTABLE || v.verdict === NOT_ACCEPTABLE);
  const decision110 = !decidable
    ? { decision: "CANNOT DECIDE", reason: `type body: ${typeBody.verdict} (${typeBody.reason}); cadence: ${jQ3Cadence.verdict} (${jQ3Cadence.reason})` }
    : typeBody.verdict === ACCEPTABLE && jQ3Cadence.verdict === ACCEPTABLE
      ? { decision: "CLOSE AS NOT-NEEDED", reason: `the fill body already emits a per-character keystroke sequence (${typeBody.reason}) and its cadence is human-plausible (${jQ3Cadence.reason}), so a conditional timing model would have nothing to add` }
      : { decision: "SHOULD OPEN", reason: `${typeBody.verdict === ACCEPTABLE ? "the fill body types per-character but" : `the fill body does not type per-character (${typeBody.reason}) and`} the cadence is ${jQ3Cadence.verdict} (${jQ3Cadence.reason})` };
  console.log("");
  console.log("--- hand-off to #110 (conditional timing model) ---------------------------------");
  console.log(`  DECISION: #110 ${decision110.decision}`);
  console.log(`  because : ${decision110.reason}`);
  console.log(`  facts   : browser_type body emitted ${perChar.keydown} keydown / ${perChar.keyup} keyup over ${TEXT.length} character(s);`);
  console.log(`            the submit:true Enter emitted ${submitShape.enterKeyEvents} key event(s) through press("Enter"), a different mechanism;`);
  console.log(`            inter-keystroke intervals available to model: ${interKeystroke ? interKeystroke.n : 0}.`);
  if (decision110.decision === "SHOULD OPEN") {
    console.log("  scope   : #110 owns the FILL BODY only. The press(\"Enter\") path already produces a real");
    console.log("            keystroke, so a timing model must not be bolted onto it a second time.");
  }
  record.decision110 = { ...decision110, typeBody: { verdict: typeBody.verdict, reason: typeBody.reason }, cadence: jQ3Cadence };
} catch (err) {
  console.error("");
  if (err instanceof InstrumentAbort) {
    // A self-check already recorded WHY and printed a FAIL line; a stack here would read like a
    // harness bug rather than the deliberate stop it is.
    console.error(`  measurement aborted: ${err.message}`);
  } else {
    console.error(`  harness error: ${err instanceof Error ? (err.stack ?? err.message) : String(err)}`);
    fail(`harness error: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`);
  }
  if (instrumentFailures === 0) instrumentFailures++;
} finally {
  if (conn) {
    // Best-effort tidy close of the drive session before tearing the transport down, so the browser
    // is released deliberately rather than by killing its parent.
    await conn.client.callTool({ name: "browser_close", arguments: {} }, undefined, { timeout: 30_000 }).catch(() => {});
    await conn.close();
  }
  // Chrome's keep-alive sockets would otherwise hold `close()` open past the browser's exit.
  server.closeAllConnections?.();
  await new Promise((r) => server.close(r));
  restoreHostsEntry(hostsState);
}

record.finishedAt = new Date().toISOString();
record.instrumentOk = instrumentFailures === 0;
if (process.env.INPUT_REALISM_OUT) {
  try {
    mkdirSync(dirname(process.env.INPUT_REALISM_OUT), { recursive: true });
    writeFileSync(process.env.INPUT_REALISM_OUT, `${JSON.stringify(record, null, 2)}\n`);
    console.log(`  json record     : ${process.env.INPUT_REALISM_OUT}`);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(`  json record     : FAILED to write ${process.env.INPUT_REALISM_OUT} — ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`);
  }
}

console.log("");
if (instrumentFailures === 0) {
  console.log("=== INSTRUMENT OK — the numbers above are the measurement (findings do not fail this run) ===");
} else {
  console.log(`=== INSTRUMENT FAILED (${instrumentFailures}) — ${record.instrumentFailures.join("; ")} ===`);
  console.log("=== The exit code reflects the INSTRUMENT, never the finding. #111 is the gate. ===");
}
// Exit explicitly: a lingering browser child or an unref'd page timer must not hold the process open
// after the measurement is printed.
process.exit(instrumentFailures === 0 ? 0 : 1);
